t      - 0.498\n673\t      - 17.0\n674\t  - name: 'Table 6: Cross-Architecture Pareto Frontier Agreement'\n675\t    columns:\n676\t    - metric\n677\t    - qwen3_235b\n678\t    - llama4_scout\n679\t    - agreement\n680\t    rows:\n681\t    - - rank_corr_depth_vs_transfer\n682\t      - 0.41\n683\t      - 0.22\n684\t      - —\n685\t    - - pareto_optimal_layer_count\n686\t      - 6/24\n687\t      - 3/12\n688\t      - —\n689\t    - - best_transfer_layer_pct_depth\n690\t      - 55%\n691\t      - 50%\n692\t      - —\n693\t    - - zs_mean_auroc\n694\t      - 0.621\n695\t      - 0.594\n696\t      - —\n697\t    - - optimal_layer_rank_agreement_tau\n698\t      - —\n699\t      - —\n700\t      - 0.33\n701\t    - - pass_lang_overlap_jaccard\n702\t      - —\n703\t      - —\n704\t      - 0.44\n705\t  figures_description:\n706\t  - 'Figure 2a: Side-by-side Pareto frontier scatter plots for Qwen3-235B (left) and\n707\t    Llama 4 Scout (right). Qwen3 shows a discernible convex Pareto front with 6 non-dominated\n708\t    points forming a curve from upper-left (high EN, low transfer) to lower-right\n709\t    (low EN, high transfer). Llama 4 shows a near-flat cloud with only 3 weakly non-dominated\n710\t    points — no meaningful tradeoff curve. The contrast is the paper''s key Figure\n711\t    2 evidence that the Pareto frontier is architecture-dependent.'\n712\t  - 'Figure 2b: Llama 4 Scout non-monotonicity detail: line plot of transfer AUROC\n713\t    vs layer depth for zh, fr, ar, sw. The zh/fr lines show a bump at layer 20, dip\n714\t    at 24, recover at 28 — violating the monotonic depth-transfer assumption. This\n715\t    is attributed to MoE expert routing transitions at these layers.'\n716\t  analysis: 'Llama 4 Scout is the worst-tier''s smoking gun for the diagnostic reframe.\n717\t    The Pareto frontier essentially doesn''t exist on this architecture (rank corr\n718\t    0.22, only 3 non-dominated layers). This is the strongest evidence for the diagnostic\n719\t    paper''s thesis: the depth-transfer tradeoff from Claim 1 is not a universal property\n720\t    of transformers but an architecture-dependent phenomenon that cannot be assumed\n721\t    to generalize. The 0.33 cross-architecture agreement means practitioners cannot\n722\t    use findings from one model to guide layer selection on another. For the paper,\n723\t    this transforms from a weakness into a key finding: the field cannot assume probe-based\n724\t    calibration will behave consistently across architectures.\n725\t\n726\t    '\n727\t- name: CaCLR Implementation and Comparative Evaluation\n728\t  plan_id: plan_003\n729\t  description: 'Implement CaCLR (L_BCE + α·L_SB-ECE + β·L_consistency). Train CaCLR\n730\t    probes using English labels + unlabeled parallel activations. Compare vs all 5\n731\t    transfer conditions across full Pareto frontier for both models × 20 languages.\n732\t\n733\t    '\n734\t  expected_results:\n735\t    caclr_auroc_mean_qwen3: 0.645\n736\t    caclr_auroc_mean_llama4: 0.618\n737\t    caclr_vs_linproj_auroc_delta_pp_qwen3: -0.3\n738\t    caclr_vs_linproj_auroc_delta_pp_llama4: -0.5\n739\t    caclr_vs_zs_auroc_delta_pp_qwen3: 2.4\n740\t    caclr_vs_zs_auroc_delta_pp_llama4: 2.4\n741\t    caclr_vs_fewshot100_auroc_delta_pp_qwen3: -2.6\n742\t    caclr_vs_fewshot100_auroc_delta_pp_llama4: -3.4\n743\t    caclr_improvement_pass_rate_qwen3: 0.47\n744\t    caclr_improvement_pass_rate_llama4: 0.37\n745\t    caclr_ece_mean_qwen3: 0.174\n746\t    caclr_ece_mean_llama4: 0.189\n747\t    caclr_ece_reduction_vs_tempcal_pct_qwen3: 7.2\n748\t    caclr_ece_reduction_vs_tempcal_pct_llama4: 3.8\n749\t    best_alpha: 0.3\n750\t    best_beta: 0.05\n751\t    alpha_sensitivity: moderate — ±0.1 changes AUROC by ~0.5pp\n752\t    beta_sensitivity: flat — β from 0.01 to 0.5 changes AUROC by <0.3pp, indicating\n753\t      consistency loss is inert\n754\t    caclr_no_consistency_auroc_mean: 0.647\n755\t    caclr_no_sbece_auroc_mean: 0.629\n756\t    caclr_bce_only_auroc_mean: 0.621\n757\t    caclr_per_lang_qwen3:\n758\t      zh:\n759\t        auroc: 0.671\n760\t        delta_vs_zs: 0.3\n761\t        delta_vs_linproj: -0.8\n762\t      fr:\n763\t        auroc: 0.668\n764\t        delta_vs_zs: -0.4\n765\t        delta_vs_linproj: -1.2\n766\t      de:\n767\t        auroc: 0.665\n768\t        delta_vs_zs: -0.6\n769\t        delta_vs_linproj: -0.9\n770\t      es:\n771\t        auroc: 0.658\n772\t        delta_vs_zs: -0.3\n773\t        delta_vs_linproj: -0.7\n774\t      ja:\n775\t        auroc: 0.649\n776\t        delta_vs_zs: -0.2\n777\t        delta_vs_linproj: -0.4\n778\t      ru:\n779\t        auroc: 0.641\n780\t        delta_vs_zs: -0.3\n781\t        delta_vs_linproj: -0.6\n782\t      ko:\n783\t        auroc: 0.647\n784\t        delta_vs_zs: -0.1\n785\t        delta_vs_linproj: -0.3\n786\t      ar:\n787\t        auroc: 0.628\n788\t        delta_vs_zs: 1.6\n789\t        delta_vs_linproj: 0.2\n790\t      hi:\n791\t        auroc: 0.635\n792\t        delta_vs_zs: 1.7\n793\t        delta_vs_linproj: 0.1\n794\t      vi:\n795\t        auroc: 0.621\n796\t        delta_vs_zs: 1.4\n797\t        delta_vs_linproj: -0.3\n798\t      id:\n799\t        auroc: 0.633\n800\t        delta_vs_zs: 1.5\n801\t        delta_vs_linproj: -0.1\n802\t      th:\n803\t        auroc: 0.607\n804\t        delta_vs_zs: 1.6\n805\t        delta_vs_linproj: 0.3\n806\t      pl:\n807\t        auroc: 0.638\n808\t        delta_vs_zs: 1.7\n809\t        delta_vs_linproj: 0.1\n810\t      uk:\n811\t        auroc: 0.631\n812\t        delta_vs_zs: 1.7\n813\t        delta_vs_linproj: 0.2\n814\t      bn:\n815\t        auroc: 0.601\n816\t        delta_vs_zs: 1.8\n817\t        delta_vs_linproj: 0.5\n818\t      te:\n819\t        auroc: 0.589\n820\t        delta_vs_zs: 1.8\n821\t        delta_vs_linproj: 0.4\n822\t      ta:\n823\t        auroc: 0.587\n824\t        delta_vs_zs: 1.9\n825\t        delta_vs_linproj: 0.6\n826\t      sw:\n827\t        auroc: 0.578\n828\t        delta_vs_zs: 2.1\n829\t        delta_vs_linproj: 0.9\n830\t      yo:\n831\t        auroc: 0.551\n832\t        delta_vs_zs: 2.2\n833\t        delta_vs_linproj: 0.7\n834\t  result_rationale: 'CaCLR''s consistency loss (L_consistency) is designed to align\n835\t    cross-lingual probe activations so that the English probe generalizes. In the\n836\t    worst tier, this fails because the cross-lingual correctness signal is too noisy\n837\t    for the consistency objective to find meaningful invariances. The unlabeled parallel\n838\t    activations contain language-specific variance (script, morphology, tokenization\n839\t    artifacts) that dominates the correctness signal, so minimizing distributional\n840\t    distance between languages collapses both signal and noise. The hyperparameter\n841\t    search confirms this: β (consistency weight) is driven to near-zero (0.05), and\n842\t    removing the consistency term entirely slightly improves performance (+0.2pp).\n843\t    The SB-ECE term helps modestly because it acts as a calibration regularizer on\n844\t    the English side, but this benefit doesn''t compound with consistency. CaCLR ends\n845\t    up being essentially \"zero-shot + SB-ECE regularization\" — functionally a slightly\n846\t    calibration-aware version of the base probe. Linear projection beats CaCLR because\n847\t    projection directly estimates and applies the affine transformation between language\n848\t    representation subspaces, which is a more efficient use of the few available parallel\n849\t    datapoints than trying to learn an invariant representation. The key insight:\n850\t    simple alignment (linproj) outperforms principled invariance (CaCLR) when the\n851\t    signal-to-noise ratio of the target signal is low. For high-resource languages\n852\t    that already transfer well, CaCLR''s overhead yields no benefit; for low-resource\n853\t    languages where help is needed, the consistency signal is too noisy to learn from.\n854\t\n855\t    '\n856\t  tables:\n857\t  - name: 'Table 7: Transfer Method Comparison Summary (19-Language Mean, Both Architectures)'\n858\t    columns:\n859\t    - method\n860\t    - qwen3_auroc\n861\t    - qwen3_ece\n862\t    - llama4_auroc\n863\t    - llama4_ece\n864\t    - labels_needed\n865\t    rows:\n866\t    - - zero_shot\n867\t      - 0.621\n868\t      - 0.194\n869\t      - 0.594\n870\t      - 0.212\n871\t      - 0 target\n872\t    - - mean_shift\n873\t      - 0.604\n874\t      - 0.225\n875\t      - 0.571\n876\t      - 0.248\n877\t      - 0 target (fails)\n878\t    - - linear_projection\n879\t      - 0.648\n880\t      - 0.171\n881\t      - 0.623\n882\t      - 0.189\n883\t      - ~50 parallel\n884\t    - - CaCLR\n885\t      - 0.645\n886\t      - 0.174\n887\t      - 0.618\n888\t      - 0.193\n889\t      - 0 target + parallel\n890\t    - - few_shot_25\n891\t      - 0.654\n892\t      - 0.166\n893\t      - 0.631\n894\t      - 0.182\n895\t      - 25 target\n896\t    - - few_shot_100\n897\t      - 0.671\n898\t      - 0.152\n899\t      - 0.652\n900\t      - 0.168\n901\t      - 100 target\n902\t  - name: 'Table 8: CaCLR Ablation (Qwen3-235B, 19-Language Mean)'\n903\t    columns:\n904\t    - variant\n905\t    - auroc_mean\n906\t    - ece_mean\n907\t    - delta_vs_full_pp\n908\t    rows:\n909\t    - - Full CaCLR (BCE+SB-ECE+Consistency)\n910\t      - 0.645\n911\t      - 0.174\n912\t      - 0.0\n913\t    - - CaCLR − Consistency (BCE+SB-ECE)\n914\t      - 0.647\n915\t      - 0.172\n916\t      - 0.2\n917\t    - - CaCLR − SB-ECE (BCE+Consistency)\n918\t      - 0.629\n919\t      - 0.188\n920\t      - -1.6\n921\t    - - BCE Only (= zero-shot probe)\n922\t      - 0.621\n923\t      - 0.194\n924\t      - -2.4\n925\t    - - Linear Projection\n926\t      - 0.648\n927\t      - 0.171\n928\t      - 0.3\n929\t  figures_description:\n930\t  - 'Figure 3a: Grouped bar chart comparing 6 transfer methods across 20 languages\n931\t    (Qwen3-235B), sorted by language resource tier. Shows that few-shot-100 > few-shot-25\n932\t    ≈ linear projection ≈ CaCLR > zero-shot >> mean-shift. CaCLR and linear projection\n933\t    bars are nearly identical in height across all languages. The visual message is\n934\t    clear: CaCLR adds complexity without benefit.'\n935\t  - 'Figure 3b: Hyperparameter sensitivity plot for CaCLR. Left panel: AUROC vs α\n936\t    (SB-ECE weight), showing a gentle inverted-U peaking at α=0.3. Right panel: AUROC\n937\t    vs β (consistency weight), showing a flat line — β has no effect. This is the\n938\t    key visual evidence that the consistency objective is inert.'\n939\t  - 'Figure 3c: Scatter plot of per-language CaCLR improvement (y-axis) vs zero-shot\n940\t    baseline AUROC (x-axis). Shows that CaCLR helps low-resource languages modestly\n941\t    (+1-2pp) but slightly hurts high-resource languages that already transfer well\n942\t    (-0.3 to -1.2pp). Linear projection shows the same pattern but with uniformly\n943\t    better outcomes. Net: CaCLR''s mechanism doesn''t add value over simple geometric\n944\t    alignment.'\n945\t  analysis: 'CaCLR is a rigorous negative result. The consistency loss term — the\n946\t    core methodological novelty — is inert. This is not a hyperparameter tuning failure;\n947\t    β is swept over 3 orders of magnitude and the landscape is flat. The SB-ECE term\n948\t    provides modest calibration regularization, but this benefit is available without\n949\t    any cross-lingual machinery. Linear projection achieves the same or better results\n950\t    with a simpler mechanism. For the paper, this is framed as: \"consistency-based\n951\t    cross-lingual alignment objectives do not help when the underlying correctness\n952\t    signal has low cross-lingual SNR. This negative result saves the community from\n953\t    pursuing distributional-matching approaches (adversarial, MMD, consistency) for\n954\t    this problem class.\"\n955\t\n956\t    '\n957\t- name: Difficulty-Controlled Ablation (Decomposition)\n958\t  plan_id: plan_004\n959\t  description: 'Train IRT difficulty probes, project out difficulty direction from\n960\t    activations, retrain correctness probes on residuals, evaluate transfer. 10 representative\n961\t    languages × 8 representative layers per model.\n962\t\n963\t    '\n964\t  expected_results:\n965\t    difficulty_probe_r2_en_qwen3: 0.52\n966\t    difficulty_probe_r2_en_llama4: 0.47\n967\t    difficulty_probe_transfer_r2_mean_9lang: 0.41\n968\t    decomp_difficulty_var_explained_pct_qwen3: 14.3\n969\t    decomp_difficulty_var_explained_pct_llama4: 11.8\n970\t    correctness_residual_auroc_en_qwen3: 0.738\n971\t    correctness_residual_auroc_en_llama4: 0.711\n972\t    correctness_residual_transfer_auroc_mean_qwen3: 0.607\n973\t    correctness_residual_transfer_auroc_mean_llama4: 0.581\n974\t    transfer_improvement_after_projection: false\n975\t    transfer_delta_after_projection_pp_qwen3: -1.4\n976\t    transfer_delta_after_projection_pp_llama4: -1.3\n977\t    difficulty_correctness_cosine_mean_qwen3: 0.34\n978\t    difficulty_correctness_cosine_mean_llama4: 0.38\n979\t    difficulty_correctness_cosine_std: 0.11\n980\t    subspace_overlap_mean_angle_deg: 58\n981\t  result_rationale: 'In the worst tier, difficulty and capability are entangled in\n982\t    cross-lingual representations. The difficulty direction explains only 14% (Qwen3)\n983\t    / 12% (Llama4) of correctness probe variance — real but modest. Critically, projecting\n984\t    out difficulty makes transfer WORSE (-1.3 to -1.4pp), not better. This is because\n985\t    the difficulty-shared component actually carries useful cross-lingual signal:\n986\t    questions that are hard in English tend to be hard everywhere due to shared conceptual\n987\t    complexity, and this shared difficulty signal is one of the few reliable cross-lingual\n988\t    bridges for the correctness probe. Removing it strips away a genuine cross-lingual\n989\t    invariance. The residual \"capability-specific\" component is more language-dependent,\n990\t    not less — it contains the idiosyncratic ways each language''s tokens encode competence,\n991\t    which are precisely the non-transferable signals. The cosine similarity of 0.34-0.38\n992\t    between difficulty and correctness directions confirms they are neither orthogonal\n993\t    nor aligned — they share a moderate subspace that cannot be cleanly separated.\n994\t    This contradicts the paper''s original Claim 2 hypothesis that decomposition would\n995\t    be prescriptive. The decomposition is real but useless: it describes variance\n996\t    structure without enabling better transfer.\n997\t\n998\t    '\n999\t  tables:\n1000\t  - name: 'Table 9: Difficulty Probe Quality (Ridge Regression, R² on Held-Out)'\n1001\t    columns:\n1002\t    - language\n1003\t    - qwen3_r2\n1004\t    - llama4_r2\n1005\t    - is_transfer\n1006\t    rows:\n1007\t    - - en\n1008\t      - 0.52\n1009\t      - 0.47\n1010\t      - false\n1011\t    - - zh\n1012\t      - 0.48\n1013\t      - 0.43\n1014\t      - true\n1015\t    - - fr\n1016\t      - 0.47\n1017\t      - 0.42\n1018\t      - true\n1019\t    - - de\n1020\t      - 0.46\n1021\t      - 0.41\n1022\t      - true\n1023\t    - - ar\n1024\t      - 0.42\n1025\t      - 0.37\n1026\t      - true\n1027\t    - - hi\n1028\t      - 0.41\n1029\t      - 0.36\n1030\t      - true\n1031\t    - - ja\n1032\t      - 0.44\n1033\t      - 0.39\n1034\t      - true\n1035\t    - - ko\n1036\t      - 0.43\n1037\t      - 0.38\n1038\t      - true\n1039\t    - - sw\n1040\t      - 0.33\n1041\t      - 0.28\n1042\t      - true\n1043\t    - - yo\n1044\t      - 0.29\n1045\t      - 0.24\n1046\t      - true\n1047\t  - name: 'Table 10: Decomposition Transfer Ablation (9-Language Mean)'\n1048\t    columns:\n1049\t    - condition\n1050\t    - qwen3_auroc\n1051\t    - qwen3_delta_pp\n1052\t    - llama4_auroc\n1053\t    - llama4_delta_pp\n1054\t    rows:\n1055\t    - - Full activations (baseline)\n1056\t      - 0.621\n1057\t      - 0.0\n1058\t      - 0.594\n1059\t      - 0.0\n1060\t    - - Residual (difficulty projected out)\n1061\t      - 0.607\n1062\t      - -1.4\n1063\t      - 0.581\n1064\t      - -1.3\n1065\t    - - Difficulty component only\n1066\t      - 0.568\n1067\t      - -5.3\n1068\t      - 0.551\n1069\t      - -4.3\n1070\t    - - Random direction projected out (ctrl)\n1071\t      - 0.618\n1072\t      - -0.3\n1073\t      - 0.591\n1074\t      - -0.3\n1075\t  figures_description:\n1076\t  - 'Figure 4a: Scatter plot of correctness AUROC vs difficulty R² across 10 languages\n1077\t    × 8 layers, colored by model. Shows moderate positive correlation (r=0.41) — layers\n1078\t    where difficulty is well-encoded also support better correctness probing. This\n1079\t    contradicts the expectation that difficulty is a confound to be removed.'\n1080\t  - 'Figure 4b: Principal angle distribution between difficulty and correctness subspaces\n1081\t    across layers. Histogram peaks at ~55-60° — far from both 0° (fully shared) and\n1082\t    90° (fully orthogonal). The wide spread (std ~11°) across layers and languages\n1083\t    shows the relationship is inconsistent, preventing clean decomposition.'\n1084\t  analysis: 'Decomposition fails as a prescriptive tool. The difficulty-shared component,\n1085\t    far from being a confound, is actually one of the more reliable cross-lingual\n1086\t    bridges — removing it hurts. The capability-specific residual is more language-dependent,\n1087\t    not less, because it captures idiosyncratic encoding patterns. For the diagnostic\n1088\t    paper, this finding is reframed as: \"difficulty and capability are entangled in\n1089\t    multilingual representations, unlike the clean separation reported in English-only\n1090\t    work. This entanglement may be fundamental to how multilingual models represent\n1091\t    knowledge — the shared difficulty structure is load-bearing for cross-lingual\n1092\t    transfer, not a confound to be controlled for.\"\n1093\t\n1094\t    '\n1095\t- name: Cross-Lingual Orthogonality Analysis\n1096\t  plan_id: plan_005\n1097\t  description: 'Extract verbalized confidence direction, compute cosine similarity\n1098\t    with correctness direction across 20 languages × 2 architectures. Analyze by resource\n1099\t    tier and language family.\n1100\t\n1101\t    '\n1102\t  expected_results:\n1103\t    orthogonality_cosine_mean_overall: 0.072\n1104\t    orthogonality_cosine_std_overall: 0.038\n1105\t    orthogonality_cosine_mean_qwen3: 0.068\n1106\t    orthogonality_cosine_mean_llama4: 0.076\n1107\t    orthogonality_cosine_range:\n1108\t    - 0.018\n1109\t    - 0.148\n1110\t    orthogonality_cosine_high_resource: 0.054\n1111\t    orthogonality_cosine_mid_resource: 0.071\n1112\t    orthogonality_cosine_low_resource: 0.098\n1113\t    pct_langs_cosine_below_0_1: 0.75\n1114\t    pct_langs_cosine_below_0_05: 0.35\n1115\t    confidence_probe_r2_mean: 0.68\n1116\t    confidence_probe_transfer_drops: true\n1117\t  result_rationale: 'Orthogonality persists cross-lingually (mean cosine 0.072, all\n1118\t    languages below 0.15) because it is a structural property of how LLMs represent\n1119\t    confidence vs correctness — the model''s verbalized confidence is calibrated to\n1120\t    appear confident regardless of actual correctness, and this dissociation is encoded\n1121\t    geometrically at the representation level. However, the worst-tier variance is\n1122\t    higher than Miao & Ungar''s English-only finding (std 0.038 vs their ~0.01): low-resource\n1123\t    languages show less orthogonality (cosine ~0.098) because the model has less capacity\n1124\t    to maintain the confidence-correctness dissociation for languages with limited\n1125\t    training data — when the model is uncertain due to lack of language-specific knowledge,\n1126\t    both its correctness and confidence signals degrade in correlated ways, slightly\n1127\t    breaking orthogonality. Reviewers may see this as incremental: Miao & Ungar showed\n1128\t    orthogonality for English, we extend to 20 languages, but the finding is qualitatively\n1129\t    the same. The resource- tier modulation is the one novel angle but the effect\n1130\t    size is modest (0.054 vs 0.098).\n1131\t\n1132\t    '\n1133\t  tables:\n1134\t  - name: 'Table 11: Correctness-Confidence Cosine Similarity by Language'\n1135\t    columns:\n1136\t    - language\n1137\t    - resource_tier\n1138\t    - qwen3_cosine\n1139\t    - llama4_cosine\n1140\t    - mean_cosine\n1141\t    rows:\n1142\t    - - en\n1143\t      - high\n1144\t      - 0.038\n1145\t      - 0.041\n1146\t      - 0.04\n1147\t    - - zh\n1148\t      - high\n1149\t      - 0.042\n1150\t      - 0.049\n1151\t      - 0.046\n1152\t    - - fr\n1153\t      - high\n1154\t      - 0.045\n1155\t      - 0.051\n1156\t      - 0.048\n1157\t    - - de\n1158\t      - high\n1159\t      - 0.047\n1160\t      - 0.054\n1161\t      - 0.051\n1162\t    - - es\n1163\t      - high\n1164\t      - 0.049\n1165\t      - 0.056\n1166\t      - 0.053\n1167\t    - - ja\n1168\t      - high\n1169\t      - 0.055\n1170\t      - 0.062\n1171\t      - 0.059\n1172\t    - - ru\n1173\t      - high\n1174\t      - 0.058\n1175\t      - 0.064\n1176\t      - 0.061\n1177\t    - - ko\n1178\t      - high\n1179\t      - 0.061\n1180\t      - 0.068\n1181\t      - 0.065\n1182\t    - - ar\n1183\t      - mid\n1184\t      - 0.063\n1185\t      - 0.072\n1186\t      - 0.068\n1187\t    - - hi\n1188\t      - mid\n1189\t      - 0.065\n1190\t      - 0.075\n1191\t      - 0.07\n1192\t    - - vi\n1193\t      - mid\n1194\t      - 0.068\n1195\t      - 0.077\n1196\t      - 0.073\n1197\t    - - id\n1198\t      - mid\n1199\t      - 0.064\n1200\t      - 0.073\n1201\t      - 0.069\n1202\t    - - th\n1203\t      - mid\n1204\t      - 0.072\n1205\t      - 0.082\n1206\t      - 0.077\n1207\t    - - pl\n1208\t      - mid\n1209\t      - 0.059\n1210\t      - 0.067\n1211\t      - 0.063\n1212\t    - - uk\n1213\t      - mid\n1214\t      - 0.062\n1215\t      - 0.071\n1216\t      - 0.067\n1217\t    - - bn\n1218\t      - low\n1219\t      - 0.081\n1220\t      - 0.092\n1221\t      - 0.087\n1222\t    - - te\n1223\t      - low\n1224\t      - 0.088\n1225\t      - 0.098\n1226\t      - 0.093\n1227\t    - - ta\n1228\t      - low\n1229\t      - 0.091\n1230\t      - 0.103\n1231\t      - 0.097\n1232\t    - - sw\n1233\t      - low\n1234\t      - 0.098\n1235\t      - 0.112\n1236\t      - 0.105\n1237\t    - - yo\n1238\t      - low\n1239\t      - 0.112\n1240\t      - 0.148\n1241\t      - 0.13\n1242\t  - name: 'Table 12: Orthogonality by Resource Tier (Mean Cosine ± Std)'\n1243\t    columns:\n1244\t    - resource_tier\n1245\t    - n_langs\n1246\t    - qwen3_mean\n1247\t    - llama4_mean\n1248\t    - overall_mean\n1249\t    - std\n1250\t    rows:\n1251\t    - - high\n1252\t      - 8\n1253\t      - 0.049\n1254\t      - 0.056\n1255\t      - 0.054\n1256\t      - 0.009\n1257\t    - - mid\n1258\t      - 7\n1259\t      - 0.065\n1260\t      - 0.074\n1261\t      - 0.071\n1262\t      - 0.005\n1263\t    - - low\n1264\t      - 5\n1265\t      - 0.094\n1266\t      - 0.111\n1267\t      - 0.098\n1268\t      - 0.016\n1269\t    - - all\n1270\t      - 20\n1271\t      - 0.066\n1272\t      - 0.076\n1273\t      - 0.072\n1274\t      - 0.022\n1275\t  figures_description:\n1276\t  - 'Figure 5a: Bar chart of cosine similarity (correctness vs confidence direction)\n1277\t    for 20 languages, sorted by resource tier, with Miao & Ungar''s English-only threshold\n1278\t    (0.04) marked as a horizontal dashed line. Most high-resource languages cluster\n1279\t    near the line; low-resource languages are 2-3× higher but still below 0.15. The\n1280\t    visual confirms orthogonality persists everywhere but the quantitative story is\n1281\t    noisier than prior work suggested.'\n1282\t  - 'Figure 5b: Scatter plot of orthogonality cosine (y-axis) vs zero-shot transfer\n1283\t    AUROC (x-axis), per language. Moderate negative correlation (r ≈ -0.62): languages\n1284\t    where orthogonality is stronger (lower cosine) also transfer better. This connects\n1285\t    Claims 1 and 4 — representation quality covaries with both metrics — but the causal\n1286\t    direction is ambiguous.'\n1287\t  analysis: 'Claim 4 survives in the worst tier but its contribution is incremental.\n1288\t    Orthogonality persists (mean 0.072, all < 0.15) — output-based calibration is\n1289\t    indeed structurally limited everywhere. The resource-tier gradient (high: 0.054,\n1290\t    mid: 0.071, low: 0.098) is novel but the effect size is modest. The correlation\n1291\t    between orthogonality and transfer success is intriguing but not causal. For the\n1292\t    diagnostic paper, this is framed as the one mechanistic takeaway: \"the correctness-confidence\n1293\t    dissociation is a multilingual phenomenon, confirming that output-based calibration\n1294\t    methods face a structural ceiling regardless of language. However, this ceiling\n1295\t    is slightly lower for low-resource languages, suggesting that the dissociation\n1296\t    partly reflects representation quality rather than a pure architectural invariant.\"\n1297\t\n1298\t    '\n1299\t- name: Probe Complexity Comparison\n1300\t  plan_id: plan_006\n1301\t  description: 'Compare logistic regression vs 2-layer MLP at each probed layer for\n1302\t    both models. Test hypothesis that linear probes transfer better cross-lingually.\n1303\t\n1304\t    '\n1305\t  expected_results:\n1306\t    mlp_vs_logreg_en_auroc_delta_mean_pp_qwen3: 1.1\n1307\t    mlp_vs_logreg_en_auroc_delta_mean_pp_llama4: 0.9\n1308\t    mlp_vs_logreg_transfer_auroc_delta_mean_pp_qwen3: -0.4\n1309\t    mlp_vs_logreg_transfer_auroc_delta_mean_pp_llama4: -0.2\n1310\t    hypothesis_linear_transfers_better: weak support\n1311\t    effect_size_cohens_d: 0.18\n1312\t    mlp_advantage_shallow_layers_pp: 0.6\n1313\t    mlp_advantage_deep_layers_pp: 1.4\n1314\t    logreg_transfer_advantage_shallow_pp: 0.1\n1315\t    logreg_transfer_advantage_deep_pp: 0.7\n1316\t  result_rationale: 'The hypothesis that linear probes transfer better receives only\n1317\t    weak support. MLP probes are ~1pp better in English because the 2-layer MLP captures\n1318\t    mild nonlinearities in the correctness signal (interaction effects between representation\n1319\t    dimensions). But the cross-lingual transfer difference is tiny (-0.2 to -0.4pp\n1320\t    favoring logistic regression) and inconsistent across languages and layers — Cohen''s\n1321\t    d of 0.18 means the effect would be invisible without large sample sizes. The\n1322\t    interaction with depth is the most interesting signal: at deep layers, MLP probes\n1323\t    overfit to English-specific patterns more (1.4pp English advantage) and consequently\n1324\t    transfer worse (0.7pp transfer penalty). But even this effect is too small to\n1325\t    be practically actionable. For the paper, this confirms logistic regression as\n1326\t    the appropriate probe choice (simpler, nearly as good) but doesn''t generate a\n1327\t    standalone finding.\n1328\t\n1329\t    '\n1330\t  tables:\n1331\t  - name: 'Table 13: Probe Complexity Comparison (Mean Across All Layers)'\n1332\t    columns:\n1333\t    - model\n1334\t    - probe\n1335\t    - en_auroc_mean\n1336\t    - transfer_auroc_mean\n1337\t    - en_ece_mean\n1338\t    - transfer_ece_mean\n1339\t    rows:\n1340\t    - - qwen3\n1341\t      - logreg\n1342\t      - 0.714\n1343\t      - 0.621\n1344\t      - 0.206\n1345\t      - 0.194\n1346\t    - - qwen3\n1347\t      - mlp\n1348\t      - 0.725\n1349\t      - 0.617\n1350\t      - 0.198\n1351\t      - 0.197\n1352\t    - - llama4\n1353\t      - logreg\n1354\t      - 0.697\n1355\t      - 0.594\n1356\t      - 0.219\n1357\t      - 0.212\n1358\t    - - llama4\n1359\t      - mlp\n1360\t      - 0.706\n1361\t      - 0.592\n1362\t      - 0.212\n1363\t      - 0.214\n1364\t  figures_description:\n1365\t  - 'Figure S1 (supplementary): Paired difference plot (MLP AUROC − LogReg AUROC)\n1366\t    vs layer depth, one panel for English (positive differences, increasing with depth)\n1367\t    and one for transfer mean (negative but near-zero differences, slightly increasing\n1368\t    magnitude with depth). The confidence bands overlap zero at most layers for the\n1369\t    transfer panel.'\n1370\t  analysis: 'Probe complexity is a non-finding. The effect is real but negligible\n1371\t    (Cohen''s d 0.18). Logistic regression is the correct default choice: nearly identical\n1372\t    performance with fewer hyperparameters and better interpretability. This goes\n1373\t    into the supplementary as a methods validation, not a main finding.\n1374\t\n1375\t    '\n1376\t- name: Additional Benchmark Evaluation (Belebele + MMLU-ProX)\n1377\t  plan_id: plan_007\n1378\t  description: 'Evaluate best probes + CaCLR on Belebele (reading comprehension, 20\n1379\t    languages) and MMLU-ProX (translated MMLU, 20 languages).\n1380\t\n1381\t    '\n1382\t  expected_results:\n1383\t    belebele_en_auroc_qwen3: 0.741\n1384\t    belebele_en_auroc_llama4: 0.718\n1385\t    belebele_zs_transfer_mean_qwen3: 0.598\n1386\t    belebele_zs_transfer_mean_llama4: 0.572\n1387\t    belebele_drop_vs_globalmmlu_pp: 2.3\n1388\t    belebele_caclr_delta_vs_linproj_pp: -0.6\n1389\t    mmlupx_en_auroc_qwen3: 0.762\n1390\t    mmlupx_en_auroc_llama4: 0.738\n1391\t    mmlupx_zs_transfer_mean_qwen3: 0.583\n1392\t    mmlupx_zs_transfer_mean_llama4: 0.557\n1393\t    mmlupx_drop_vs_globalmmlu_pp: 3.8\n1394\t    mmlupx_caclr_delta_vs_linproj_pp: -0.9\n1395\t    lang_rank_correlation_globalmmlu_vs_belebele: 0.82\n1396\t    lang_rank_correlation_globalmmlu_vs_mmlupx: 0.71\n1397\t  result_rationale: 'Belebele partially replicates GlobalMMLU findings because it\n1398\t    is also a high-quality, natively multilingual benchmark where translation artifacts\n1399\t    are minimal. The 2.3pp drop reflects the domain shift (reading comprehension vs\n1400\t    knowledge questions) but the relative ranking of languages is preserved (rank\n1401\t    corr 0.82). MMLU-ProX shows worse transfer (3.8pp drop) because it is machine-translated\n1402\t    from English MMLU — translation artifacts create systematic distributional shifts\n1403\t    that the English-trained correctness probe cannot handle. The translation introduces\n1404\t    new failure modes: ambiguous translations for domain-specific terms, cultural\n1405\t    references that don''t transfer, and occasional errors in low-resource translations.\n1406\t    CaCLR is again no better than linear projection on either benchmark, confirming\n1407\t    the negative result generalizes beyond the training benchmark.\n1408\t\n1409\t    '\n1410\t  tables:\n1411\t  - name: 'Table 14: Cross-Benchmark Generalization (19-Language Mean)'\n1412\t    columns:\n1413\t    - benchmark\n1414\t    - metric\n1415\t    - qwen3\n1416\t    - llama4\n1417\t    rows:\n1418\t    - - GlobalMMLU\n1419\t      - zs_transfer_auroc\n1420\t      - 0.621\n1421\t      - 0.594\n1422\t    - - Belebele\n1423\t      - zs_transfer_auroc\n1424\t      - 0.598\n1425\t      - 0.572\n1426\t    - - MMLU-ProX\n1427\t      - zs_transfer_auroc\n1428\t      - 0.583\n1429\t      - 0.557\n1430\t    - - GlobalMMLU\n1431\t      - caclr_vs_linproj_pp\n1432\t      - -0.3\n1433\t      - -0.5\n1434\t    - - Belebele\n1435\t      - caclr_vs_linproj_pp\n1436\t      - -0.6\n1437\t      - -0.8\n1438\t    - - MMLU-ProX\n1439\t      - caclr_vs_linproj_pp\n1440\t      - -0.9\n1441\t      - -1.2\n1442\t  figures_description:\n1443\t  - 'Figure S2 (supplementary): Three-panel scatter plot showing per-language transfer\n1444\t    AUROC correlation across benchmark pairs (GlobalMMLU vs Belebele, GlobalMMLU vs\n1445\t    MMLU-ProX, Belebele vs MMLU-ProX). High correlation for the first pair, moderate\n1446\t    for others. Low-resource languages (marked in red) are consistent outliers on\n1447\t    MMLU-ProX due to translation quality.'\n1448\t  analysis: 'Results partially generalize: Belebele confirms the main findings, MMLU-ProX\n1449\t    is noisier due to translation artifacts. The language ranking is stable across\n1450\t    benchmarks (which languages transfer well vs poorly is consistent), but absolute\n1451\t    performance degrades with benchmark domain shift. CaCLR''s negative result is\n1452\t    robust across all three benchmarks — it never beats linear projection. For the\n1453\t    paper, include Belebele in the main results and MMLU-ProX in supplementary with\n1454\t    a note about translation artifacts.\n1455\t\n1456\t    '\n1457\t- name: INT8 Validation Ablation\n1458\t  plan_id: plan_010\n1459\t  description: 'Compare INT8 vs FP16 probing results at 5 representative layers for\n1460\t    Llama 4 Scout on 5% random subset. Validate that INT8 quantization doesn''t distort\n1461\t    correctness signals.\n1462\t\n1463\t    '\n1464\t  expected_results:\n1465\t    int8_vs_fp16_auroc_delta_mean_pp: -0.3\n1466\t    int8_vs_fp16_auroc_delta_max_pp: -0.7\n1467\t    int8_vs_fp16_ece_delta_mean_pp: 0.2\n1468\t    int8_vs_fp16_activation_cosine_sim_mean: 0.994\n1469\t    int8_vs_fp16_rank_correlation_layers: 0.98\n1470\t    validation_passes: true\n1471\t  result_rationale: 'INT8 quantization introduces minimal distortion to correctness\n1472\t    signals. The 0.3pp mean AUROC difference is within noise — INT8 preserves the\n1473\t    relative ordering of activations that linear probes rely on, even though absolute\n1474\t    magnitudes shift slightly. The 0.994 cosine similarity between INT8 and FP16 activations\n1475\t    confirms near-perfect directional alignment. This is expected because correctness\n1476\t    is encoded in the direction of activations, not their magnitude, and INT8 preserves\n1477\t    directions well. The layer ranking is identical (rank corr 0.98). This ablation\n1478\t    passes cleanly even in the worst tier — quantization artifacts are not the source\n1479\t    of any failures observed elsewhere.\n1480\t\n1481\t    '\n1482\t  tables:\n1483\t  - name: 'Table S1 (supplementary): INT8 vs FP16 Probing Comparison'\n1484\t    columns:\n1485\t    - layer\n1486\t    - fp16_auroc\n1487\t    - int8_auroc\n1488\t    - delta_pp\n1489\t    - activation_cosine\n1490\t    rows:\n1491\t    - - 8\n1492\t      - 0.637\n1493\t      - 0.633\n1494\t      - -0.4\n1495\t      - 0.993\n1496\t    - - 16\n1497\t      - 0.7\n1498\t      - 0.697\n1499\t      - -0.3\n1500\t      - 0.994\n1501\t    - - 24\n1502\t      - 0.731\n1503\t      - 0.729\n1504\t      - -0.2\n1505\t      - 0.995\n1506\t    - - 32\n1507\t      - 0.746\n1508\t      - 0.743\n1509\t      - -0.3\n1510\t      - 0.994\n1511\t    - - 40\n1512\t      - 0.748\n1513\t      - 0.741\n1514\t      - -0.7\n1515\t      - 0.992\n1516\t  figures_description:\n1517\t  - 'Figure S3 (supplementary): Paired scatter plot of FP16 vs INT8 AUROC at each\n1518\t    layer × language combination. Points lie tightly along the y=x diagonal with slight\n1519\t    downward bias. Inset shows histogram of deltas, centered near -0.3pp with tight\n1520\t    spread.'\n1521\t  analysis: 'INT8 validation passes. Quantization is not a confound for any findings\n1522\t    in this paper. Include in supplementary appendix as one paragraph + Table S1.\n1523\t\n1524\t    '\n1525\t- name: Gemma 4 31B Dense — Third Architecture (Camera-Ready)\n1526\t  plan_id: plan_008\n1527\t  description: 'Full Pareto frontier on Gemma 4 31B Dense (60 layers, dense architecture,\n1528\t    FP16). Provides dense-vs-MoE architectural contrast for camera-ready extension.\n1529\t\n1530\t    '\n1531\t  expected_results:\n1532\t    en_auroc_best_layer: 0.761\n1533\t    en_best_layer_idx: 45\n1534\t    zs_transfer_auroc_mean_19lang: 0.608\n1535\t    zs_transfer_drop_mean_pp: 16.8\n1536\t    pareto_depth_transfer_rank_corr: 0.48\n1537\t    pareto_frontier_agreement_with_qwen3: 0.42\n1538\t    pareto_frontier_agreement_with_llama4: 0.29\n1539\t    caclr_vs_linproj_pp: -0.2\n1540\t    dense_vs_moe_finding: Dense architecture shows cleaner Pareto frontier (rank corr\n1541\t      0.48 vs 0.41/0.22 for MoE models) — MoE routing is a source of non-monotonic\n1542\t      noise in the depth-transfer relationship\n1543\t  result_rationale: 'Gemma 4 31B Dense shows a cleaner (but still moderate) Pareto\n1544\t    frontier because dense architectures have more homogeneous layer-to-layer representation\n1545\t    transformations — no expert routing to create non-monotonic jumps. The rank correlation\n1546\t    of 0.48 is the highest of the three architectures but still in the worst tier\n1547\t    range. English AUROC of 0.761 is between Qwen3 (0.776) and Llama4 (0.749), consistent\n1548\t    with its intermediate model size. Transfer is intermediate too (0.608). Cross-architecture\n1549\t    agreement remains low (0.42 with Qwen3, 0.29 with Llama4), confirming architecture-dependence.\n1550\t    The dense-vs-MoE contrast is the main value-add for camera-ready: it demonstrates\n1551\t    that MoE routing is a specific source of frontier noise, not just model variation.\n1552\t\n1553\t    '\n1554\t  tables:\n1555\t  - name: 'Table 15: Three-Architecture Comparison Summary'\n1556\t    columns:\n1557\t    - model\n1558\t    - type\n1559\t    - layers\n1560\t    - en_auroc\n1561\t    - transfer_auroc\n1562\t    - pareto_rank_corr\n1563\t    - pareto_agreement_mean\n1564\t    rows:\n1565\t    - - Qwen3-235B\n1566\t      - MoE\n1567\t      - 94\n1568\t      - 0.776\n1569\t      - 0.621\n1570\t      - 0.41\n1571\t      - 0.38\n1572\t    - - Llama4-Scout\n1573\t      - MoE\n1574\t      - 48\n1575\t      - 0.749\n1576\t      - 0.594\n1577\t      - 0.22\n1578\t      - 0.31\n1579\t    - - Gemma4-31B\n1580\t      - Dense\n1581\t      - 60\n1582\t      - 0.761\n1583\t      - 0.608\n1584\t      - 0.48\n1585\t      - 0.36\n1586\t  figures_description:\n1587\t  - 'Figure 6 (camera-ready): Three-panel Pareto frontier comparison (Qwen3, Llama4,\n1588\t    Gemma4). The dense model''s frontier is the most visually coherent, with points\n1589\t    forming a recognizable curve. MoE models show increasing scatter. Annotated with\n1590\t    rank correlation values.'\n1591\t  analysis: 'Gemma4 confirms the diagnostic paper''s thesis from a new angle: the\n1592\t    Pareto frontier is more coherent in dense architectures, implicating MoE routing\n1593\t    as a specific source of cross-lingual transfer noise. For camera-ready, this strengthens\n1594\t    the \"architecture matters\" message without changing the core worst-tier narrative.\n1595\t\n1596\t    '\n1597\t- name: Extended Language Coverage (50+ Languages, Camera-Ready)\n1598\t  plan_id: plan_009\n1599\t  description: 'Expand from 20 to 50+ languages using full GlobalMMLU/Belebele language\n1600\t    sets.\n1601\t\n1602\t    '\n1603\t  expected_results:\n1604\t    total_languages: 52\n1605\t    zs_transfer_auroc_mean_51lang: 0.587\n1606\t    zs_pass_rate_51lang: 0.235\n1607\t    new_lang_families_added: 5\n1608\t    low_resource_auroc_mean_new_langs: 0.548\n1609\t    worst_language_auroc: 0.491\n1610\t    resource_tier_gradient_steeper: true\n1611\t  result_rationale: 'Expanding to 50+ languages worsens the aggregate picture because\n1612\t    the additional languages are predominantly low- and very-low-resource. Many of\n1613\t    these languages (e.g., Amharic, Lao, Mongolian, Georgian) have minimal representation\n1614\t    in the models'' training data, so their internal representations share even less\n1615\t    structure with English. The pass rate drops from ~37% (7/19) to ~24% (12/51),\n1616\t    and some languages approach chance (AUROC ~0.49). For the paper, this strengthens\n1617\t    the diagnostic message: \"probe-based calibration has a severe language barrier\n1618\t    that scales with the number of languages you try to cover.\"\n1619\t\n1620\t    '\n1621\t  tables:\n1622\t  - name: 'Table 16: Transfer Success by Resource Tier (Expanded 52-Language Set,\n1623\t      Qwen3)'\n1624\t    columns:\n1625\t    - resource_tier\n1626\t    - n_langs\n1627\t    - zs_auroc_mean\n1628\t    - zs_auroc_std\n1629\t    - pass_rate\n1630\t    rows:\n1631\t    - - high\n1632\t      - 10\n1633\t      - 0.653\n1634\t      - 0.018\n1635\t      - 0.7\n1636\t    - - mid\n1637\t      - 18\n1638\t      - 0.603\n1639\t      - 0.024\n1640\t      - 0.28\n1641\t    - - low\n1642\t      - 14\n1643\t      - 0.558\n1644\t      - 0.031\n1645\t      - 0.07\n1646\t    - - very_low\n1647\t      - 10\n1648\t      - 0.521\n1649\t      - 0.022\n1650\t      - 0.0\n1651\t  figures_description:\n1652\t  - 'Figure 7 (camera-ready): World map heatmap with 52 languages colored by zero-shot\n1653\t    transfer AUROC. Europe and East Asia are green/yellow (adequate transfer). South/Southeast\n1654\t    Asia is orange. Sub-Saharan Africa and Central Asia are red (near-chance). Visual\n1655\t    reinforces the ''language barrier'' framing.'\n1656\t  analysis: 'Extended coverage is the nail in the coffin for universal probe-based\n1657\t    calibration. With 52 languages, the majority fail to transfer. The resource-tier\n1658\t    gradient is steep and monotonic. This is the strongest evidence for the diagnostic\n1659\t    paper''s conclusion: \"this research line does not generalize beyond a small set\n1660\t    of high-resource languages without fundamental methodological advances.\"'\n1661\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c3372ce2-16de-4b98-b026-560c840f0560","uuid":"7f9697f6-27cf-411a-b142-1e14d6ca05cf","timestamp":"2026-05-20T20:31:54.554Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0519_2/projects/xling_calib_direction/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T185641/worst/imagined_experiments.yaml","content":"tier: worst\nnarrative_guidance: 'Full reframe to field-level diagnostic paper: ''Does probe-based\n  calibration work beyond English? A systematic study across 2 architectures and 20\n  languages.'' Value proposition shifts from method contribution to first large-scale\n  answer on whether this research line generalizes. CaCLR is reported as a rigorous\n  negative result — consistency-based objectives do not help when the cross-lingual\n  correctness signal is this noisy. Decomposition is reported as evidence that difficulty\n  and capability are entangled in multilingual representations, contradicting the\n  clean separation assumed by English-only prior work. Pareto frontier is real on\n  Qwen3 but fails to replicate cleanly on Llama 4 Scout — reported as architecture-dependence\n  finding rather than universal law. Orthogonality (Claim 4) survives as the one mechanistic\n  takeaway: output-based calibration is structurally limited everywhere, not just\n  English. Target EACL with framing that negative/diagnostic results at this scale\n  (2 models × 20 languages × all layers) are community-valuable when the field is\n  actively expanding probe-based methods without multilingual validation.\n\n  '\nimagined_experiments:\n- name: Full Pareto Frontier — Qwen3-235B-A22B (94 layers, MoE, INT8)\n  plan_id: plan_001\n  description: 'Extract activations at 24 probed layers (every 4th of 94), 20 languages\n    on GlobalMMLU. Train English logistic regression + 2-layer MLP correctness probes.\n    Evaluate 5 transfer conditions: zero-shot, mean-shift, linear projection, few-shot\n    (25/100), CaCLR.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    en_auroc_best_layer: 0.776\n    en_best_layer_idx: 72\n    en_auroc_range:\n    - 0.612\n    - 0.776\n    en_ece_raw_best: 0.172\n    en_ece_tempcal_best: 0.068\n    temp_scaling_T: 5.83\n    zs_transfer_auroc_mean_19lang: 0.621\n    zs_transfer_auroc_median_19lang: 0.633\n    zs_transfer_auroc_std_19lang: 0.054\n    zs_transfer_drop_mean_pp: 15.5\n    zs_pass_rate_19lang: 0.368\n    zs_pass_langs:\n    - zh\n    - fr\n    - de\n    - es\n    - ja\n    - ru\n    - ko\n    zs_fail_langs:\n    - ar\n    - hi\n    - sw\n    - yo\n    - te\n    - bn\n    - ta\n    - th\n    - vi\n    - uk\n    - id\n    - pl\n    mean_shift_improves_count: 0\n    mean_shift_mean_delta_ece_pp: 3.14\n    linproj_auroc_mean_19lang: 0.648\n    linproj_improves_over_zs_count: 13\n    linproj_mean_delta_auroc_vs_zs_pp: 2.7\n    fewshot25_auroc_mean_19lang: 0.654\n    fewshot25_improves_over_zs_count: 14\n    fewshot100_auroc_mean_19lang: 0.671\n    fewshot100_improves_over_zs_count: 16\n    pareto_depth_transfer_rank_corr: 0.41\n    pareto_frontier_monotonic: false\n    pareto_n_dominated_layers: 6\n    transfer_by_family:\n      indo_european_germanic:\n        mean: 0.672\n        langs:\n        - de\n        - en\n      indo_european_romance:\n        mean: 0.661\n        langs:\n        - fr\n        - es\n      indo_european_slavic:\n        mean: 0.629\n        langs:\n        - ru\n        - uk\n        - pl\n      indo_european_indic:\n        mean: 0.598\n        langs:\n        - hi\n        - bn\n        - te\n        - ta\n      sino_tibetan:\n        mean: 0.668\n        langs:\n        - zh\n      japonic:\n        mean: 0.651\n        langs:\n        - ja\n      koreanic:\n        mean: 0.648\n        langs:\n        - ko\n      afroasiatic:\n        mean: 0.612\n        langs:\n        - ar\n      niger_congo:\n        mean: 0.543\n        langs:\n        - sw\n        - yo\n      tai_kadai:\n        mean: 0.591\n        langs:\n        - th\n      austronesian:\n        mean: 0.618\n        langs:\n        - id\n      austroasiatic:\n        mean: 0.607\n        langs:\n        - vi\n  result_rationale: 'Qwen3-235B is a much larger MoE model than the MVP''s Qwen2.5-7B.\n    English AUROC at 0.776 improves modestly over MVP''s 0.754 because the model is\n    more capable and the correctness signal is more linearly accessible in larger\n    models, but gains are sublinear — the larger model also has more complex, distributed\n    representations that resist simple linear probing. Zero-shot transfer mean of\n    0.621 reflects the worst-tier prediction that transfer is unreliable: high-resource\n    Indo-European languages (fr, de, es) and CJK (zh, ja, ko) transfer adequately\n    because their representations share more subspace structure with English at intermediate\n    layers, but low-resource languages (sw, yo, te, bn) and languages with very different\n    morphology (th, ta) fail because the MoE routing creates language-specific expert\n    patterns that are opaque to an English-trained linear probe. Mean-shift fails\n    entirely (0/19) because the mean activation difference between languages conflates\n    content, style, and script differences — subtracting it removes useful signal.\n    The Pareto frontier rank correlation of 0.41 is real but moderate: the general\n    trend (deeper = better English, worse transfer) holds, but MoE routing introduces\n    non-monotonic noise at mid-depth layers where expert activation patterns shift.\n    The 7/19 pass rate reflects that only typologically close or high-resource languages\n    have enough representational overlap with English for zero-shot transfer to work.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 1: Qwen3-235B English Probe Performance by Layer (Selected Layers)'\n    columns:\n    - layer\n    - depth_pct\n    - logreg_auroc\n    - mlp_auroc\n    - ece_raw\n    - ece_tempcal\n    rows:\n    - - 4\n      - 4\n      - 0.612\n      - 0.618\n      - 0.298\n      - 0.192\n    - - 16\n      - 17\n      - 0.653\n      - 0.661\n      - 0.251\n      - 0.148\n    - - 28\n      - 30\n      - 0.694\n      - 0.708\n      - 0.224\n      - 0.121\n    - - 40\n      - 43\n      - 0.721\n      - 0.737\n      - 0.203\n      - 0.098\n    - - 52\n      - 55\n      - 0.742\n      - 0.755\n      - 0.191\n      - 0.082\n    - - 60\n      - 64\n      - 0.758\n      - 0.769\n      - 0.183\n      - 0.075\n    - - 72\n      - 77\n      - 0.776\n      - 0.783\n      - 0.172\n      - 0.068\n    - - 80\n      - 85\n      - 0.771\n      - 0.779\n      - 0.176\n      - 0.071\n    - - 88\n      - 94\n      - 0.753\n      - 0.762\n      - 0.185\n      - 0.079\n    - - 92\n      - 98\n      - 0.728\n      - 0.739\n      - 0.198\n      - 0.091\n  - name: 'Table 2: Qwen3-235B Zero-Shot Transfer AUROC by Language (Best Transfer\n      Layer)'\n    columns:\n    - language\n    - family\n    - resource_tier\n    - best_zs_layer\n    - best_zs_auroc\n    - en_auroc_at_same_layer\n    - drop_pp\n    rows:\n    - - zh\n      - sino_tibetan\n      - high\n      - 52\n      - 0.668\n      - 0.742\n      - 7.4\n    - - fr\n      - romance\n      - high\n      - 52\n      - 0.672\n      - 0.742\n      - 7.0\n    - - de\n      - germanic\n      - high\n      - 52\n      - 0.671\n      - 0.742\n      - 7.1\n    - - es\n      - romance\n      - high\n      - 52\n      - 0.661\n      - 0.742\n      - 8.1\n    - - ja\n      - japonic\n      - high\n      - 48\n      - 0.651\n      - 0.735\n      - 8.4\n    - - ru\n      - slavic\n      - high\n      - 48\n      - 0.644\n      - 0.735\n      - 9.1\n    - - ko\n      - koreanic\n      - high\n      - 48\n      - 0.648\n      - 0.735\n      - 8.7\n    - - ar\n      - afroasiatic\n      - mid\n      - 40\n      - 0.612\n      - 0.721\n      - 10.9\n    - - hi\n      - indic\n      - mid\n      - 40\n      - 0.618\n      - 0.721\n      - 10.3\n    - - vi\n      - austroasiatic\n      - mid\n      - 40\n      - 0.607\n      - 0.721\n      - 11.4\n    - - id\n      - austronesian\n      - mid\n      - 40\n      - 0.618\n      - 0.721\n      - 10.3\n    - - th\n      - tai_kadai\n      - mid\n      - 40\n      - 0.591\n      - 0.721\n      - 13.0\n    - - pl\n      - slavic\n      - mid\n      - 40\n      - 0.621\n      - 0.721\n      - 10.0\n    - - uk\n      - slavic\n      - mid\n      - 40\n      - 0.614\n      - 0.721\n      - 10.7\n    - - bn\n      - indic\n      - low\n      - 28\n      - 0.583\n      - 0.694\n      - 11.1\n    - - te\n      - indic\n      - low\n      - 28\n      - 0.571\n      - 0.694\n      - 12.3\n    - - ta\n      - indic\n      - low\n      - 28\n      - 0.568\n      - 0.694\n      - 12.6\n    - - sw\n      - niger_congo\n      - low\n      - 28\n      - 0.557\n      - 0.694\n      - 13.7\n    - - yo\n      - niger_congo\n      - low\n      - 28\n      - 0.529\n      - 0.694\n      - 16.5\n  - name: 'Table 3: Qwen3-235B Transfer Method Comparison (AUROC, 19-Language Mean)'\n    columns:\n    - method\n    - auroc_mean\n    - auroc_std\n    - pass_rate\n    - ece_mean\n    - delta_vs_zs_pp\n    rows:\n    - - zero_shot\n      - 0.621\n      - 0.054\n      - 0.368\n      - 0.194\n      - 0.0\n    - - mean_shift\n      - 0.604\n      - 0.067\n      - 0.263\n      - 0.225\n      - -1.7\n    - - linear_projection\n      - 0.648\n      - 0.048\n      - 0.526\n      - 0.171\n      - 2.7\n    - - few_shot_25\n      - 0.654\n      - 0.045\n      - 0.526\n      - 0.166\n      - 3.3\n    - - few_shot_100\n      - 0.671\n      - 0.041\n      - 0.632\n      - 0.152\n      - 5.0\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure 1a: Heatmap of zero-shot transfer AUROC (y-axis: 24 probed layers sorted\n    by depth, x-axis: 20 languages sorted by resource tier). Shows a gradient where\n    upper-left (deep layers × high-resource langs) has moderate AUROC (~0.65-0.67)\n    and lower-right (shallow layers × low-resource langs) is near chance (~0.52-0.57).\n    But the pattern is noisy — no crisp diagonal boundary. Several mid-depth layers\n    show unexpected dips (layers 48-56) breaking monotonicity.'\n  - 'Figure 1b: Pareto frontier scatter plot (x-axis: mean 19-lang zero-shot AUROC,\n    y-axis: English AUROC at same layer). Each point is a layer. The Pareto front\n    connects 6 non-dominated layers but the remaining 18 points scatter widely around\n    it. Convex hull is thin — the tradeoff is real but the frontier offers limited\n    choice. Error bars (bootstrap 95% CI) overlap substantially between adjacent Pareto-optimal\n    points.'\n  - 'Figure 1c: Line plot of zero-shot transfer drop (EN AUROC minus target-lang AUROC)\n    vs layer depth, one line per language, colored by resource tier. High-resource\n    languages (blue) show a gentle upward slope (deeper = worse transfer). Low-resource\n    languages (red) show a steep upward slope. The spread between lines increases\n    with depth, illustrating that the Pareto tradeoff is more severe for low-resource\n    languages.'\n  analysis: 'The Pareto frontier is real on Qwen3-235B but weak: rank correlation\n    of 0.41 means the depth-transfer tradeoff explains only ~17% of variance. MoE\n    routing creates non-monotonic perturbations at mid-depth. The practical implication\n    is limited — the frontier provides modest guidance for layer selection but is\n    not a reliable engineering tool. Zero-shot transfer fails for 12/19 languages\n    (AUROC < 0.65), with a clear resource-tier gradient. The field should not assume\n    English-trained correctness probes transfer to low-resource languages. Mean-shift\n    is confirmed dead at scale. Linear projection and few-shot are the only viable\n    transfer methods, with few-shot-100 achieving the best results — but requiring\n    100 labeled examples per language partially negates the zero-label promise.\n\n    '\n- name: Full Pareto Frontier — Llama 4 Scout (48 layers, MoE, INT8)\n  plan_id: plan_002\n  description: 'Extract activations at 12 probed layers (every 4th of 48), 20 languages\n    on GlobalMMLU. Train English correctness probes, evaluate 5 transfer conditions.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    en_auroc_best_layer: 0.749\n    en_best_layer_idx: 36\n    en_auroc_range:\n    - 0.598\n    - 0.749\n    en_ece_raw_best: 0.188\n    en_ece_tempcal_best: 0.074\n    temp_scaling_T: 5.47\n    zs_transfer_auroc_mean_19lang: 0.594\n    zs_transfer_auroc_median_19lang: 0.601\n    zs_transfer_auroc_std_19lang: 0.061\n    zs_transfer_drop_mean_pp: 18.2\n    zs_pass_rate_19lang: 0.211\n    zs_pass_langs:\n    - zh\n    - fr\n    - de\n    - es\n    zs_fail_langs:\n    - ja\n    - ru\n    - ko\n    - ar\n    - hi\n    - sw\n    - yo\n    - te\n    - bn\n    - ta\n    - th\n    - vi\n    - uk\n    - id\n    - pl\n    mean_shift_improves_count: 0\n    mean_shift_mean_delta_ece_pp: 4.21\n    linproj_auroc_mean_19lang: 0.623\n    linproj_improves_over_zs_count: 11\n    fewshot25_auroc_mean_19lang: 0.631\n    fewshot100_auroc_mean_19lang: 0.652\n    pareto_depth_transfer_rank_corr: 0.22\n    pareto_frontier_monotonic: false\n    pareto_n_dominated_layers: 3\n    pareto_frontier_agreement_with_qwen3: 0.33\n    nonmonotonic_layers:\n    - 20\n    - 24\n    - 28\n  result_rationale: 'Llama 4 Scout is the worst-tier''s key failure point. Its Pareto\n    frontier is flat/non-monotonic (rank correlation 0.22) because Llama 4 Scout''s\n    MoE architecture uses a different routing strategy than Qwen3-235B: its experts\n    are more language-specialized at intermediate layers, meaning the English correctness\n    direction at layers 20-28 is entangled with English-specific expert activations\n    in ways that neither degrade nor improve monotonically with depth. English AUROC\n    peaks at 0.749 (lower than Qwen3''s 0.776) because Llama 4 Scout''s 48 layers\n    compress the representational hierarchy — the same functional stages are packed\n    into fewer layers, leaving less room for a clean correctness signal to emerge.\n    Zero-shot transfer is worse overall (mean 0.594 vs Qwen3''s 0.621) because Llama\n    4 Scout''s training data distribution is more English-centric, and its MoE routing\n    assigns non-English tokens to a sparser expert set that shares less structure\n    with the English experts. Only 4/19 languages pass the 0.65 threshold. The 0.33\n    cross-architecture Pareto agreement means the optimal layer for a given language\n    on Qwen3 does not predict the optimal layer on Llama 4 — the tradeoff is architecture-specific,\n    not a universal property of transformer depth.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 4: Llama 4 Scout English Probe Performance by Layer'\n    columns:\n    - layer\n    - depth_pct\n    - logreg_auroc\n    - mlp_auroc\n    - ece_raw\n    - ece_tempcal\n    rows:\n    - - 4\n      - 8\n      - 0.598\n      - 0.604\n      - 0.312\n      - 0.201\n    - - 8\n      - 17\n      - 0.631\n      - 0.641\n      - 0.274\n      - 0.168\n    - - 12\n      - 25\n      - 0.668\n      - 0.679\n      - 0.241\n      - 0.137\n    - - 16\n      - 33\n      - 0.694\n      - 0.706\n      - 0.218\n      - 0.112\n    - - 20\n      - 42\n      - 0.712\n      - 0.722\n      - 0.205\n      - 0.098\n    - - 24\n      - 50\n      - 0.726\n      - 0.735\n      - 0.196\n      - 0.088\n    - - 28\n      - 58\n      - 0.734\n      - 0.743\n      - 0.192\n      - 0.083\n    - - 32\n      - 67\n      - 0.741\n      - 0.749\n      - 0.189\n      - 0.078\n    - - 36\n      - 75\n      - 0.749\n      - 0.755\n      - 0.188\n      - 0.074\n    - - 40\n      - 83\n      - 0.744\n      - 0.751\n      - 0.19\n      - 0.077\n    - - 44\n      - 92\n      - 0.731\n      - 0.738\n      - 0.195\n      - 0.084\n    - - 48\n      - 100\n      - 0.708\n      - 0.718\n      - 0.209\n      - 0.097\n  - name: 'Table 5: Llama 4 Scout Zero-Shot Transfer AUROC by Language (Best Transfer\n      Layer)'\n    columns:\n    - language\n    - best_zs_layer\n    - best_zs_auroc\n    - drop_vs_en_pp\n    rows:\n    - - zh\n      - 24\n      - 0.658\n      - 6.8\n    - - fr\n      - 24\n      - 0.662\n      - 6.4\n    - - de\n      - 24\n      - 0.655\n      - 7.1\n    - - es\n      - 24\n      - 0.651\n      - 7.5\n    - - ja\n      - 20\n      - 0.631\n      - 8.1\n    - - ru\n      - 20\n      - 0.622\n      - 9.0\n    - - ko\n      - 20\n      - 0.627\n      - 8.5\n    - - ar\n      - 16\n      - 0.589\n      - 10.5\n    - - hi\n      - 16\n      - 0.594\n      - 10.0\n    - - vi\n      - 16\n      - 0.581\n      - 11.3\n    - - id\n      - 16\n      - 0.591\n      - 10.3\n    - - th\n      - 16\n      - 0.568\n      - 12.6\n    - - pl\n      - 20\n      - 0.608\n      - 10.4\n    - - uk\n      - 16\n      - 0.592\n      - 10.2\n    - - bn\n      - 12\n      - 0.556\n      - 11.2\n    - - te\n      - 12\n      - 0.541\n      - 12.7\n    - - ta\n      - 12\n      - 0.538\n      - 13.0\n    - - sw\n      - 12\n      - 0.524\n      - 14.4\n    - - yo\n      - 12\n      - 0.498\n      - 17.0\n  - name: 'Table 6: Cross-Architecture Pareto Frontier Agreement'\n    columns:\n    - metric\n    - qwen3_235b\n    - llama4_scout\n    - agreement\n    rows:\n    - - rank_corr_depth_vs_transfer\n      - 0.41\n      - 0.22\n      - —\n    - - pareto_optimal_layer_count\n      - 6/24\n      - 3/12\n      - —\n    - - best_transfer_layer_pct_depth\n      - 55%\n      - 50%\n      - —\n    - - zs_mean_auroc\n      - 0.621\n      - 0.594\n      - —\n    - - optimal_layer_rank_agreement_tau\n      - —\n      - —\n      - 0.33\n    - - pass_lang_overlap_jaccard\n      - —\n      - —\n      - 0.44\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure 2a: Side-by-side Pareto frontier scatter plots for Qwen3-235B (left) and\n    Llama 4 Scout (right). Qwen3 shows a discernible convex Pareto front with 6 non-dominated\n    points forming a curve from upper-left (high EN, low transfer) to lower-right\n    (low EN, high transfer). Llama 4 shows a near-flat cloud with only 3 weakly non-dominated\n    points — no meaningful tradeoff curve. The contrast is the paper''s key Figure\n    2 evidence that the Pareto frontier is architecture-dependent.'\n  - 'Figure 2b: Llama 4 Scout non-monotonicity detail: line plot of transfer AUROC\n    vs layer depth for zh, fr, ar, sw. The zh/fr lines show a bump at layer 20, dip\n    at 24, recover at 28 — violating the monotonic depth-transfer assumption. This\n    is attributed to MoE expert routing transitions at these layers.'\n  analysis: 'Llama 4 Scout is the worst-tier''s smoking gun for the diagnostic reframe.\n    The Pareto frontier essentially doesn''t exist on this architecture (rank corr\n    0.22, only 3 non-dominated layers). This is the strongest evidence for the diagnostic\n    paper''s thesis: the depth-transfer tradeoff from Claim 1 is not a universal property\n    of transformers but an architecture-dependent phenomenon that cannot be assumed\n    to generalize. The 0.33 cross-architecture agreement means practitioners cannot\n    use findings from one model to guide layer selection on another. For the paper,\n    this transforms from a weakness into a key finding: the field cannot assume probe-based\n    calibration will behave consistently across architectures.\n\n    '\n- name: CaCLR Implementation and Comparative Evaluation\n  plan_id: plan_003\n  description: 'Implement CaCLR (L_BCE + α·L_SB-ECE + β·L_consistency). Train CaCLR\n    probes using English labels + unlabeled parallel activations. Compare vs all 5\n    transfer conditions across full Pareto frontier for both models × 20 languages.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    caclr_auroc_mean_qwen3: 0.645\n    caclr_auroc_mean_llama4: 0.618\n    caclr_vs_linproj_auroc_delta_pp_qwen3: -0.3\n    caclr_vs_linproj_auroc_delta_pp_llama4: -0.5\n    caclr_vs_zs_auroc_delta_pp_qwen3: 2.4\n    caclr_vs_zs_auroc_delta_pp_llama4: 2.4\n    caclr_vs_fewshot100_auroc_delta_pp_qwen3: -2.6\n    caclr_vs_fewshot100_auroc_delta_pp_llama4: -3.4\n    caclr_improvement_pass_rate_qwen3: 0.47\n    caclr_improvement_pass_rate_llama4: 0.37\n    caclr_ece_mean_qwen3: 0.174\n    caclr_ece_mean_llama4: 0.189\n    caclr_ece_reduction_vs_tempcal_pct_qwen3: 7.2\n    caclr_ece_reduction_vs_tempcal_pct_llama4: 3.8\n    best_alpha: 0.3\n    best_beta: 0.05\n    alpha_sensitivity: moderate — ±0.1 changes AUROC by ~0.5pp\n    beta_sensitivity: flat — β from 0.01 to 0.5 changes AUROC by <0.3pp, indicating\n      consistency loss is inert\n    caclr_no_consistency_auroc_mean: 0.647\n    caclr_no_sbece_auroc_mean: 0.629\n    caclr_bce_only_auroc_mean: 0.621\n    caclr_per_lang_qwen3:\n      zh:\n        auroc: 0.671\n        delta_vs_zs: 0.3\n        delta_vs_linproj: -0.8\n      fr:\n        auroc: 0.668\n        delta_vs_zs: -0.4\n        delta_vs_linproj: -1.2\n      de:\n        auroc: 0.665\n        delta_vs_zs: -0.6\n        delta_vs_linproj: -0.9\n      es:\n        auroc: 0.658\n        delta_vs_zs: -0.3\n        delta_vs_linproj: -0.7\n      ja:\n        auroc: 0.649\n        delta_vs_zs: -0.2\n        delta_vs_linproj: -0.4\n      ru:\n        auroc: 0.641\n        delta_vs_zs: -0.3\n        delta_vs_linproj: -0.6\n      ko:\n        auroc: 0.647\n        delta_vs_zs: -0.1\n        delta_vs_linproj: -0.3\n      ar:\n        auroc: 0.628\n        delta_vs_zs: 1.6\n        delta_vs_linproj: 0.2\n      hi:\n        auroc: 0.635\n        delta_vs_zs: 1.7\n        delta_vs_linproj: 0.1\n      vi:\n        auroc: 0.621\n        delta_vs_zs: 1.4\n        delta_vs_linproj: -0.3\n      id:\n        auroc: 0.633\n        delta_vs_zs: 1.5\n        delta_vs_linproj: -0.1\n      th:\n        auroc: 0.607\n        delta_vs_zs: 1.6\n        delta_vs_linproj: 0.3\n      pl:\n        auroc: 0.638\n        delta_vs_zs: 1.7\n        delta_vs_linproj: 0.1\n      uk:\n        auroc: 0.631\n        delta_vs_zs: 1.7\n        delta_vs_linproj: 0.2\n      bn:\n        auroc: 0.601\n        delta_vs_zs: 1.8\n        delta_vs_linproj: 0.5\n      te:\n        auroc: 0.589\n        delta_vs_zs: 1.8\n        delta_vs_linproj: 0.4\n      ta:\n        auroc: 0.587\n        delta_vs_zs: 1.9\n        delta_vs_linproj: 0.6\n      sw:\n        auroc: 0.578\n        delta_vs_zs: 2.1\n        delta_vs_linproj: 0.9\n      yo:\n        auroc: 0.551\n        delta_vs_zs: 2.2\n        delta_vs_linproj: 0.7\n  result_rationale: 'CaCLR''s consistency loss (L_consistency) is designed to align\n    cross-lingual probe activations so that the English probe generalizes. In the\n    worst tier, this fails because the cross-lingual correctness signal is too noisy\n    for the consistency objective to find meaningful invariances. The unlabeled parallel\n    activations contain language-specific variance (script, morphology, tokenization\n    artifacts) that dominates the correctness signal, so minimizing distributional\n    distance between languages collapses both signal and noise. The hyperparameter\n    search confirms this: β (consistency weight) is driven to near-zero (0.05), and\n    removing the consistency term entirely slightly improves performance (+0.2pp).\n    The SB-ECE term helps modestly because it acts as a calibration regularizer on\n    the English side, but this benefit doesn''t compound with consistency. CaCLR ends\n    up being essentially \"zero-shot + SB-ECE regularization\" — functionally a slightly\n    calibration-aware version of the base probe. Linear projection beats CaCLR because\n    projection directly estimates and applies the affine transformation between language\n    representation subspaces, which is a more efficient use of the few available parallel\n    datapoints than trying to learn an invariant representation. The key insight:\n    simple alignment (linproj) outperforms principled invariance (CaCLR) when the\n    signal-to-noise ratio of the target signal is low. For high-resource languages\n    that already transfer well, CaCLR''s overhead yields no benefit; for low-resource\n    languages where help is needed, the consistency signal is too noisy to learn from.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 7: Transfer Method Comparison Summary (19-Language Mean, Both Architectures)'\n    columns:\n    - method\n    - qwen3_auroc\n    - qwen3_ece\n    - llama4_auroc\n    - llama4_ece\n    - labels_needed\n    rows:\n    - - zero_shot\n      - 0.621\n      - 0.194\n      - 0.594\n      - 0.212\n      - 0 target\n    - - mean_shift\n      - 0.604\n      - 0.225\n      - 0.571\n      - 0.248\n      - 0 target (fails)\n    - - linear_projection\n      - 0.648\n      - 0.171\n      - 0.623\n      - 0.189\n      - ~50 parallel\n    - - CaCLR\n      - 0.645\n      - 0.174\n      - 0.618\n      - 0.193\n      - 0 target + parallel\n    - - few_shot_25\n      - 0.654\n      - 0.166\n      - 0.631\n      - 0.182\n      - 25 target\n    - - few_shot_100\n      - 0.671\n      - 0.152\n      - 0.652\n      - 0.168\n      - 100 target\n  - name: 'Table 8: CaCLR Ablation (Qwen3-235B, 19-Language Mean)'\n    columns:\n    - variant\n    - auroc_mean\n    - ece_mean\n    - delta_vs_full_pp\n    rows:\n    - - Full CaCLR (BCE+SB-ECE+Consistency)\n      - 0.645\n      - 0.174\n      - 0.0\n    - - CaCLR − Consistency (BCE+SB-ECE)\n      - 0.647\n      - 0.172\n      - 0.2\n    - - CaCLR − SB-ECE (BCE+Consistency)\n      - 0.629\n      - 0.188\n      - -1.6\n    - - BCE Only (= zero-shot probe)\n      - 0.621\n      - 0.194\n      - -2.4\n    - - Linear Projection\n      - 0.648\n      - 0.171\n      - 0.3\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure 3a: Grouped bar chart comparing 6 transfer methods across 20 languages\n    (Qwen3-235B), sorted by language resource tier. Shows that few-shot-100 > few-shot-25\n    ≈ linear projection ≈ CaCLR > zero-shot >> mean-shift. CaCLR and linear projection\n    bars are nearly identical in height across all languages. The visual message is\n    clear: CaCLR adds complexity without benefit.'\n  - 'Figure 3b: Hyperparameter sensitivity plot for CaCLR. Left panel: AUROC vs α\n    (SB-ECE weight), showing a gentle inverted-U peaking at α=0.3. Right panel: AUROC\n    vs β (consistency weight), showing a flat line — β has no effect. This is the\n    key visual evidence that the consistency objective is inert.'\n  - 'Figure 3c: Scatter plot of per-language CaCLR improvement (y-axis) vs zero-shot\n    baseline AUROC (x-axis). Shows that CaCLR helps low-resource languages modestly\n    (+1-2pp) but slightly hurts high-resource languages that already transfer well\n    (-0.3 to -1.2pp). Linear projection shows the same pattern but with uniformly\n    better outcomes. Net: CaCLR''s mechanism doesn''t add value over simple geometric\n    alignment.'\n  analysis: 'CaCLR is a rigorous negative result. The consistency loss term — the\n    core methodological novelty — is inert. This is not a hyperparameter tuning failure;\n    β is swept over 3 orders of magnitude and the landscape is flat. The SB-ECE term\n    provides modest calibration regularization, but this benefit is available without\n    any cross-lingual machinery. Linear projection achieves the same or better results\n    with a simpler mechanism. For the paper, this is framed as: \"consistency-based\n    cross-lingual alignment objectives do not help when the underlying correctness\n    signal has low cross-lingual SNR. This negative result saves the community from\n    pursuing distributional-matching approaches (adversarial, MMD, consistency) for\n    this problem class.\"\n\n    '\n- name: Difficulty-Controlled Ablation (Decomposition)\n  plan_id: plan_004\n  description: 'Train IRT difficulty probes, project out difficulty direction from\n    activations, retrain correctness probes on residuals, evaluate transfer. 10 representative\n    languages × 8 representative layers per model.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    difficulty_probe_r2_en_qwen3: 0.52\n    difficulty_probe_r2_en_llama4: 0.47\n    difficulty_probe_transfer_r2_mean_9lang: 0.41\n    decomp_difficulty_var_explained_pct_qwen3: 14.3\n    decomp_difficulty_var_explained_pct_llama4: 11.8\n    correctness_residual_auroc_en_qwen3: 0.738\n    correctness_residual_auroc_en_llama4: 0.711\n    correctness_residual_transfer_auroc_mean_qwen3: 0.607\n    correctness_residual_transfer_auroc_mean_llama4: 0.581\n    transfer_improvement_after_projection: false\n    transfer_delta_after_projection_pp_qwen3: -1.4\n    transfer_delta_after_projection_pp_llama4: -1.3\n    difficulty_correctness_cosine_mean_qwen3: 0.34\n    difficulty_correctness_cosine_mean_llama4: 0.38\n    difficulty_correctness_cosine_std: 0.11\n    subspace_overlap_mean_angle_deg: 58\n  result_rationale: 'In the worst tier, difficulty and capability are entangled in\n    cross-lingual representations. The difficulty direction explains only 14% (Qwen3)\n    / 12% (Llama4) of correctness probe variance — real but modest. Critically, projecting\n    out difficulty makes transfer WORSE (-1.3 to -1.4pp), not better. This is because\n    the difficulty-shared component actually carries useful cross-lingual signal:\n    questions that are hard in English tend to be hard everywhere due to shared conceptual\n    complexity, and this shared difficulty signal is one of the few reliable cross-lingual\n    bridges for the correctness probe. Removing it strips away a genuine cross-lingual\n    invariance. The residual \"capability-specific\" component is more language-dependent,\n    not less — it contains the idiosyncratic ways each language''s tokens encode competence,\n    which are precisely the non-transferable signals. The cosine similarity of 0.34-0.38\n    between difficulty and correctness directions confirms they are neither orthogonal\n    nor aligned — they share a moderate subspace that cannot be cleanly separated.\n    This contradicts the paper''s original Claim 2 hypothesis that decomposition would\n    be prescriptive. The decomposition is real but useless: it describes variance\n    structure without enabling better transfer.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 9: Difficulty Probe Quality (Ridge Regression, R² on Held-Out)'\n    columns:\n    - language\n    - qwen3_r2\n    - llama4_r2\n    - is_transfer\n    rows:\n    - - en\n      - 0.52\n      - 0.47\n      - false\n    - - zh\n      - 0.48\n      - 0.43\n      - true\n    - - fr\n      - 0.47\n      - 0.42\n      - true\n    - - de\n      - 0.46\n      - 0.41\n      - true\n    - - ar\n      - 0.42\n      - 0.37\n      - true\n    - - hi\n      - 0.41\n      - 0.36\n      - true\n    - - ja\n      - 0.44\n      - 0.39\n      - true\n    - - ko\n      - 0.43\n      - 0.38\n      - true\n    - - sw\n      - 0.33\n      - 0.28\n      - true\n    - - yo\n      - 0.29\n      - 0.24\n      - true\n  - name: 'Table 10: Decomposition Transfer Ablation (9-Language Mean)'\n    columns:\n    - condition\n    - qwen3_auroc\n    - qwen3_delta_pp\n    - llama4_auroc\n    - llama4_delta_pp\n    rows:\n    - - Full activations (baseline)\n      - 0.621\n      - 0.0\n      - 0.594\n      - 0.0\n    - - Residual (difficulty projected out)\n      - 0.607\n      - -1.4\n      - 0.581\n      - -1.3\n    - - Difficulty component only\n      - 0.568\n      - -5.3\n      - 0.551\n      - -4.3\n    - - Random direction projected out (ctrl)\n      - 0.618\n      - -0.3\n      - 0.591\n      - -0.3\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure 4a: Scatter plot of correctness AUROC vs difficulty R² across 10 languages\n    × 8 layers, colored by model. Shows moderate positive correlation (r=0.41) — layers\n    where difficulty is well-encoded also support better correctness probing. This\n    contradicts the expectation that difficulty is a confound to be removed.'\n  - 'Figure 4b: Principal angle distribution between difficulty and correctness subspaces\n    across layers. Histogram peaks at ~55-60° — far from both 0° (fully shared) and\n    90° (fully orthogonal). The wide spread (std ~11°) across layers and languages\n    shows the relationship is inconsistent, preventing clean decomposition.'\n  analysis: 'Decomposition fails as a prescriptive tool. The difficulty-shared component,\n    far from being a confound, is actually one of the more reliable cross-lingual\n    bridges — removing it hurts. The capability-specific residual is more language-dependent,\n    not less, because it captures idiosyncratic encoding patterns. For the diagnostic\n    paper, this finding is reframed as: \"difficulty and capability are entangled in\n    multilingual representations, unlike the clean separation reported in English-only\n    work. This entanglement may be fundamental to how multilingual models represent\n    knowledge — the shared difficulty structure is load-bearing for cross-lingual\n    transfer, not a confound to be controlled for.\"\n\n    '\n- name: Cross-Lingual Orthogonality Analysis\n  plan_id: plan_005\n  description: 'Extract verbalized confidence direction, compute cosine similarity\n    with correctness direction across 20 languages × 2 architectures. Analyze by resource\n    tier and language family.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    orthogonality_cosine_mean_overall: 0.072\n    orthogonality_cosine_std_overall: 0.038\n    orthogonality_cosine_mean_qwen3: 0.068\n    orthogonality_cosine_mean_llama4: 0.076\n    orthogonality_cosine_range:\n    - 0.018\n    - 0.148\n    orthogonality_cosine_high_resource: 0.054\n    orthogonality_cosine_mid_resource: 0.071\n    orthogonality_cosine_low_resource: 0.098\n    pct_langs_cosine_below_0_1: 0.75\n    pct_langs_cosine_below_0_05: 0.35\n    confidence_probe_r2_mean: 0.68\n    confidence_probe_transfer_drops: true\n  result_rationale: 'Orthogonality persists cross-lingually (mean cosine 0.072, all\n    languages below 0.15) because it is a structural property of how LLMs represent\n    confidence vs correctness — the model''s verbalized confidence is calibrated to\n    appear confident regardless of actual correctness, and this dissociation is encoded\n    geometrically at the representation level. However, the worst-tier variance is\n    higher than Miao & Ungar''s English-only finding (std 0.038 vs their ~0.01): low-resource\n    languages show less orthogonality (cosine ~0.098) because the model has less capacity\n    to maintain the confidence-correctness dissociation for languages with limited\n    training data — when the model is uncertain due to lack of language-specific knowledge,\n    both its correctness and confidence signals degrade in correlated ways, slightly\n    breaking orthogonality. Reviewers may see this as incremental: Miao & Ungar showed\n    orthogonality for English, we extend to 20 languages, but the finding is qualitatively\n    the same. The resource- tier modulation is the one novel angle but the effect\n    size is modest (0.054 vs 0.098).\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 11: Correctness-Confidence Cosine Similarity by Language'\n    columns:\n    - language\n    - resource_tier\n    - qwen3_cosine\n    - llama4_cosine\n    - mean_cosine\n    rows:\n    - - en\n      - high\n      - 0.038\n      - 0.041\n      - 0.04\n    - - zh\n      - high\n      - 0.042\n      - 0.049\n      - 0.046\n    - - fr\n      - high\n      - 0.045\n      - 0.051\n      - 0.048\n    - - de\n      - high\n      - 0.047\n      - 0.054\n      - 0.051\n    - - es\n      - high\n      - 0.049\n      - 0.056\n      - 0.053\n    - - ja\n      - high\n      - 0.055\n      - 0.062\n      - 0.059\n    - - ru\n      - high\n      - 0.058\n      - 0.064\n      - 0.061\n    - - ko\n      - high\n      - 0.061\n      - 0.068\n      - 0.065\n    - - ar\n      - mid\n      - 0.063\n      - 0.072\n      - 0.068\n    - - hi\n      - mid\n      - 0.065\n      - 0.075\n      - 0.07\n    - - vi\n      - mid\n      - 0.068\n      - 0.077\n      - 0.073\n    - - id\n      - mid\n      - 0.064\n      - 0.073\n      - 0.069\n    - - th\n      - mid\n      - 0.072\n      - 0.082\n      - 0.077\n    - - pl\n      - mid\n      - 0.059\n      - 0.067\n      - 0.063\n    - - uk\n      - mid\n      - 0.062\n      - 0.071\n      - 0.067\n    - - bn\n      - low\n      - 0.081\n      - 0.092\n      - 0.087\n    - - te\n      - low\n      - 0.088\n      - 0.098\n      - 0.093\n    - - ta\n      - low\n      - 0.091\n      - 0.103\n      - 0.097\n    - - sw\n      - low\n      - 0.098\n      - 0.112\n      - 0.105\n    - - yo\n      - low\n      - 0.112\n      - 0.148\n      - 0.13\n  - name: 'Table 12: Orthogonality by Resource Tier (Mean Cosine ± Std)'\n    columns:\n    - resource_tier\n    - n_langs\n    - qwen3_mean\n    - llama4_mean\n    - overall_mean\n    - std\n    rows:\n    - - high\n      - 8\n      - 0.049\n      - 0.056\n      - 0.054\n      - 0.009\n    - - mid\n      - 7\n      - 0.065\n      - 0.074\n      - 0.071\n      - 0.005\n    - - low\n      - 5\n      - 0.094\n      - 0.111\n      - 0.098\n      - 0.016\n    - - all\n      - 20\n      - 0.066\n      - 0.076\n      - 0.072\n      - 0.022\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure 5a: Bar chart of cosine similarity (correctness vs confidence direction)\n    for 20 languages, sorted by resource tier, with Miao & Ungar''s English-only threshold\n    (0.04) marked as a horizontal dashed line. Most high-resource languages cluster\n    near the line; low-resource languages are 2-3× higher but still below 0.15. The\n    visual confirms orthogonality persists everywhere but the quantitative story is\n    noisier than prior work suggested.'\n  - 'Figure 5b: Scatter plot of orthogonality cosine (y-axis) vs zero-shot transfer\n    AUROC (x-axis), per language. Moderate negative correlation (r ≈ -0.62): languages\n    where orthogonality is stronger (lower cosine) also transfer better. This connects\n    Claims 1 and 4 — representation quality covaries with both metrics — but the causal\n    direction is ambiguous.'\n  analysis: 'Claim 4 survives in the worst tier but its contribution is incremental.\n    Orthogonality persists (mean 0.072, all < 0.15) — output-based calibration is\n    indeed structurally limited everywhere. The resource-tier gradient (high: 0.054,\n    mid: 0.071, low: 0.098) is novel but the effect size is modest. The correlation\n    between orthogonality and transfer success is intriguing but not causal. For the\n    diagnostic paper, this is framed as the one mechanistic takeaway: \"the correctness-confidence\n    dissociation is a multilingual phenomenon, confirming that output-based calibration\n    methods face a structural ceiling regardless of language. However, this ceiling\n    is slightly lower for low-resource languages, suggesting that the dissociation\n    partly reflects representation quality rather than a pure architectural invariant.\"\n\n    '\n- name: Probe Complexity Comparison\n  plan_id: plan_006\n  description: 'Compare logistic regression vs 2-layer MLP at each probed layer for\n    both models. Test hypothesis that linear probes transfer better cross-lingually.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    mlp_vs_logreg_en_auroc_delta_mean_pp_qwen3: 1.1\n    mlp_vs_logreg_en_auroc_delta_mean_pp_llama4: 0.9\n    mlp_vs_logreg_transfer_auroc_delta_mean_pp_qwen3: -0.4\n    mlp_vs_logreg_transfer_auroc_delta_mean_pp_llama4: -0.2\n    hypothesis_linear_transfers_better: weak support\n    effect_size_cohens_d: 0.18\n    mlp_advantage_shallow_layers_pp: 0.6\n    mlp_advantage_deep_layers_pp: 1.4\n    logreg_transfer_advantage_shallow_pp: 0.1\n    logreg_transfer_advantage_deep_pp: 0.7\n  result_rationale: 'The hypothesis that linear probes transfer better receives only\n    weak support. MLP probes are ~1pp better in English because the 2-layer MLP captures\n    mild nonlinearities in the correctness signal (interaction effects between representation\n    dimensions). But the cross-lingual transfer difference is tiny (-0.2 to -0.4pp\n    favoring logistic regression) and inconsistent across languages and layers — Cohen''s\n    d of 0.18 means the effect would be invisible without large sample sizes. The\n    interaction with depth is the most interesting signal: at deep layers, MLP probes\n    overfit to English-specific patterns more (1.4pp English advantage) and consequently\n    transfer worse (0.7pp transfer penalty). But even this effect is too small to\n    be practically actionable. For the paper, this confirms logistic regression as\n    the appropriate probe choice (simpler, nearly as good) but doesn''t generate a\n    standalone finding.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 13: Probe Complexity Comparison (Mean Across All Layers)'\n    columns:\n    - model\n    - probe\n    - en_auroc_mean\n    - transfer_auroc_mean\n    - en_ece_mean\n    - transfer_ece_mean\n    rows:\n    - - qwen3\n      - logreg\n      - 0.714\n      - 0.621\n      - 0.206\n      - 0.194\n    - - qwen3\n      - mlp\n      - 0.725\n      - 0.617\n      - 0.198\n      - 0.197\n    - - llama4\n      - logreg\n      - 0.697\n      - 0.594\n      - 0.219\n      - 0.212\n    - - llama4\n      - mlp\n      - 0.706\n      - 0.592\n      - 0.212\n      - 0.214\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure S1 (supplementary): Paired difference plot (MLP AUROC − LogReg AUROC)\n    vs layer depth, one panel for English (positive differences, increasing with depth)\n    and one for transfer mean (negative but near-zero differences, slightly increasing\n    magnitude with depth). The confidence bands overlap zero at most layers for the\n    transfer panel.'\n  analysis: 'Probe complexity is a non-finding. The effect is real but negligible\n    (Cohen''s d 0.18). Logistic regression is the correct default choice: nearly identical\n    performance with fewer hyperparameters and better interpretability. This goes\n    into the supplementary as a methods validation, not a main finding.\n\n    '\n- name: Additional Benchmark Evaluation (Belebele + MMLU-ProX)\n  plan_id: plan_007\n  description: 'Evaluate best probes + CaCLR on Belebele (reading comprehension, 20\n    languages) and MMLU-ProX (translated MMLU, 20 languages).\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    belebele_en_auroc_qwen3: 0.741\n    belebele_en_auroc_llama4: 0.718\n    belebele_zs_transfer_mean_qwen3: 0.598\n    belebele_zs_transfer_mean_llama4: 0.572\n    belebele_drop_vs_globalmmlu_pp: 2.3\n    belebele_caclr_delta_vs_linproj_pp: -0.6\n    mmlupx_en_auroc_qwen3: 0.762\n    mmlupx_en_auroc_llama4: 0.738\n    mmlupx_zs_transfer_mean_qwen3: 0.583\n    mmlupx_zs_transfer_mean_llama4: 0.557\n    mmlupx_drop_vs_globalmmlu_pp: 3.8\n    mmlupx_caclr_delta_vs_linproj_pp: -0.9\n    lang_rank_correlation_globalmmlu_vs_belebele: 0.82\n    lang_rank_correlation_globalmmlu_vs_mmlupx: 0.71\n  result_rationale: 'Belebele partially replicates GlobalMMLU findings because it\n    is also a high-quality, natively multilingual benchmark where translation artifacts\n    are minimal. The 2.3pp drop reflects the domain shift (reading comprehension vs\n    knowledge questions) but the relative ranking of languages is preserved (rank\n    corr 0.82). MMLU-ProX shows worse transfer (3.8pp drop) because it is machine-translated\n    from English MMLU — translation artifacts create systematic distributional shifts\n    that the English-trained correctness probe cannot handle. The translation introduces\n    new failure modes: ambiguous translations for domain-specific terms, cultural\n    references that don''t transfer, and occasional errors in low-resource translations.\n    CaCLR is again no better than linear projection on either benchmark, confirming\n    the negative result generalizes beyond the training benchmark.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 14: Cross-Benchmark Generalization (19-Language Mean)'\n    columns:\n    - benchmark\n    - metric\n    - qwen3\n    - llama4\n    rows:\n    - - GlobalMMLU\n      - zs_transfer_auroc\n      - 0.621\n      - 0.594\n    - - Belebele\n      - zs_transfer_auroc\n      - 0.598\n      - 0.572\n    - - MMLU-ProX\n      - zs_transfer_auroc\n      - 0.583\n      - 0.557\n    - - GlobalMMLU\n      - caclr_vs_linproj_pp\n      - -0.3\n      - -0.5\n    - - Belebele\n      - caclr_vs_linproj_pp\n      - -0.6\n      - -0.8\n    - - MMLU-ProX\n      - caclr_vs_linproj_pp\n      - -0.9\n      - -1.2\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure S2 (supplementary): Three-panel scatter plot showing per-language transfer\n    AUROC correlation across benchmark pairs (GlobalMMLU vs Belebele, GlobalMMLU vs\n    MMLU-ProX, Belebele vs MMLU-ProX). High correlation for the first pair, moderate\n    for others. Low-resource languages (marked in red) are consistent outliers on\n    MMLU-ProX due to translation quality.'\n  analysis: 'Results partially generalize: Belebele confirms the main findings, MMLU-ProX\n    is noisier due to translation artifacts. The language ranking is stable across\n    benchmarks (which languages transfer well vs poorly is consistent), but absolute\n    performance degrades with benchmark domain shift. CaCLR''s negative result is\n    robust across all three benchmarks — it never beats linear projection. For the\n    paper, include Belebele in the main results and MMLU-ProX in supplementary with\n    a note about translation artifacts.\n\n    '\n- name: INT8 Validation Ablation\n  plan_id: plan_010\n  description: 'Compare INT8 vs FP16 probing results at 5 representative layers for\n    Llama 4 Scout on 5% random subset. Validate that INT8 quantization doesn''t distort\n    correctness signals.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    int8_vs_fp16_auroc_delta_mean_pp: -0.3\n    int8_vs_fp16_auroc_delta_max_pp: -0.7\n    int8_vs_fp16_ece_delta_mean_pp: 0.2\n    int8_vs_fp16_activation_cosine_sim_mean: 0.994\n    int8_vs_fp16_rank_correlation_layers: 0.98\n    validation_passes: true\n  result_rationale: 'INT8 quantization introduces minimal distortion to correctness\n    signals. The 0.3pp mean AUROC difference is within noise — INT8 preserves the\n    relative ordering of activations that linear probes rely on, even though absolute\n    magnitudes shift slightly. The 0.994 cosine similarity between INT8 and FP16 activations\n    confirms near-perfect directional alignment. This is expected because correctness\n    is encoded in the direction of activations, not their magnitude, and INT8 preserves\n    directions well. The layer ranking is identical (rank corr 0.98). This ablation\n    passes cleanly even in the worst tier — quantization artifacts are not the source\n    of any failures observed elsewhere.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table S1 (supplementary): INT8 vs FP16 Probing Comparison'\n    columns:\n    - layer\n    - fp16_auroc\n    - int8_auroc\n    - delta_pp\n    - activation_cosine\n    rows:\n    - - 8\n      - 0.637\n      - 0.633\n      - -0.4\n      - 0.993\n    - - 16\n      - 0.7\n      - 0.697\n      - -0.3\n      - 0.994\n    - - 24\n      - 0.731\n      - 0.729\n      - -0.2\n      - 0.995\n    - - 32\n      - 0.746\n      - 0.743\n      - -0.3\n      - 0.994\n    - - 40\n      - 0.748\n      - 0.741\n      - -0.7\n      - 0.992\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure S3 (supplementary): Paired scatter plot of FP16 vs INT8 AUROC at each\n    layer × language combination. Points lie tightly along the y=x diagonal with slight\n    downward bias. Inset shows histogram of deltas, centered near -0.3pp with tight\n    spread.'\n  analysis: 'INT8 validation passes. Quantization is not a confound for any findings\n    in this paper. Include in supplementary appendix as one paragraph + Table S1.\n\n    '\n- name: Gemma 4 31B Dense — Third Architecture (Camera-Ready)\n  plan_id: plan_008\n  description: 'Full Pareto frontier on Gemma 4 31B Dense (60 layers, dense architecture,\n    FP16). Provides dense-vs-MoE architectural contrast for camera-ready extension.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    en_auroc_best_layer: 0.761\n    en_best_layer_idx: 45\n    zs_transfer_auroc_mean_19lang: 0.608\n    zs_transfer_drop_mean_pp: 16.8\n    pareto_depth_transfer_rank_corr: 0.48\n    pareto_frontier_agreement_with_qwen3: 0.42\n    pareto_frontier_agreement_with_llama4: 0.29\n    caclr_vs_linproj_pp: -0.2\n    dense_vs_moe_finding: Dense architecture shows cleaner Pareto frontier (rank corr\n      0.48 vs 0.41/0.22 for MoE models) — MoE routing is a source of non-monotonic\n      noise in the depth-transfer relationship\n  result_rationale: 'Gemma 4 31B Dense shows a cleaner (but still moderate) Pareto\n    frontier because dense architectures have more homogeneous layer-to-layer representation\n    transformations — no expert routing to create non-monotonic jumps. The rank correlation\n    of 0.48 is the highest of the three architectures but still in the worst tier\n    range. English AUROC of 0.761 is between Qwen3 (0.776) and Llama4 (0.749), consistent\n    with its intermediate model size. Transfer is intermediate too (0.608). Cross-architecture\n    agreement remains low (0.42 with Qwen3, 0.29 with Llama4), confirming architecture-dependence.\n    The dense-vs-MoE contrast is the main value-add for camera-ready: it demonstrates\n    that MoE routing is a specific source of frontier noise, not just model variation.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 15: Three-Architecture Comparison Summary'\n    columns:\n    - model\n    - type\n    - layers\n    - en_auroc\n    - transfer_auroc\n    - pareto_rank_corr\n    - pareto_agreement_mean\n    rows:\n    - - Qwen3-235B\n      - MoE\n      - 94\n      - 0.776\n      - 0.621\n      - 0.41\n      - 0.38\n    - - Llama4-Scout\n      - MoE\n      - 48\n      - 0.749\n      - 0.594\n      - 0.22\n      - 0.31\n    - - Gemma4-31B\n      - Dense\n      - 60\n      - 0.761\n      - 0.608\n      - 0.48\n      - 0.36\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure 6 (camera-ready): Three-panel Pareto frontier comparison (Qwen3, Llama4,\n    Gemma4). The dense model''s frontier is the most visually coherent, with points\n    forming a recognizable curve. MoE models show increasing scatter. Annotated with\n    rank correlation values.'\n  analysis: 'Gemma4 confirms the diagnostic paper''s thesis from a new angle: the\n    Pareto frontier is more coherent in dense architectures, implicating MoE routing\n    as a specific source of cross-lingual transfer noise. For camera-ready, this strengthens\n    the \"architecture matters\" message without changing the core worst-tier narrative.\n\n    '\n- name: Extended Language Coverage (50+ Languages, Camera-Ready)\n  plan_id: plan_009\n  description: 'Expand from 20 to 50+ languages using full GlobalMMLU/Belebele language\n    sets.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    total_languages: 52\n    zs_transfer_auroc_mean_51lang: 0.587\n    zs_pass_rate_51lang: 0.235\n    new_lang_families_added: 5\n    low_resource_auroc_mean_new_langs: 0.548\n    worst_language_auroc: 0.491\n    resource_tier_gradient_steeper: true\n  result_rationale: 'Expanding to 50+ languages worsens the aggregate picture because\n    the additional languages are predominantly low- and very-low-resource. Many of\n    these languages (e.g., Amharic, Lao, Mongolian, Georgian) have minimal representation\n    in the models'' training data, so their internal representations share even less\n    structure with English. The pass rate drops from ~37% (7/19) to ~24% (12/51),\n    and some languages approach chance (AUROC ~0.49). For the paper, this strengthens\n    the diagnostic message: \"probe-based calibration has a severe language barrier\n    that scales with the number of languages you try to cover.\"\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 16: Transfer Success by Resource Tier (Expanded 52-Language Set,\n      Qwen3)'\n    columns:\n    - resource_tier\n    - n_langs\n    - zs_auroc_mean\n    - zs_auroc_std\n    - pass_rate\n    rows:\n    - - high\n      - 10\n      - 0.653\n      - 0.018\n      - 0.7\n    - - mid\n      - 18\n      - 0.603\n      - 0.024\n      - 0.28\n    - - low\n      - 14\n      - 0.558\n      - 0.031\n      - 0.07\n    - - very_low\n      - 10\n      - 0.521\n      - 0.022\n      - 0.0\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure 7 (camera-ready): World map heatmap with 52 languages colored by zero-shot\n    transfer AUROC. Europe and East Asia are green/yellow (adequate transfer). South/Southeast\n    Asia is orange. Sub-Saharan Africa and Central Asia are red (near-chance). Visual\n    reinforces the ''language barrier'' framing.'\n  analysis: 'Extended coverage is the nail in the coffin for universal probe-based\n    calibration. With 52 languages, the majority fail to transfer. The resource-tier\n    gradient is steep and monotonic. This is the strongest evidence for the diagnostic\n    paper''s conclusion: \"this research line does not generalize beyond a small set\n    of high-resource languages without fundamental methodological advances.\"'\n","numLines":1661,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1661}}}
